[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60475637-df2c-5099-a5a7-9cd23687c0fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312747} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd5a890-600f-5ed0-9892-4e5fc04515a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5c352abb-1992-52cb-94e7-b48e48f5bbc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-340110" :question/problem "Given the function $y=4{e}^{x}-3{\\log }_{2}⁡x+7,$ the slope of the tangent line at $x=3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{x}-3{\\log }_{2}⁡x+7) \\\\  & =4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\log }_{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7) \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡2}+0 \\\\  & =4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\ln ⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=3} & ={(4{e}^{x}-\\frac{3}{x\\ln ⁡2})|}_{x=3} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{3}{3\\cdot \\ln ⁡2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{\\ln ⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314916 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae0cf059-0b67-5d07-b9cb-ef0b700b4b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313336} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9df9452-cfe9-5dd4-9e79-2b944e370a96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "45b578e0-8138-5641-b677-427c5be958c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-340124" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=1\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot 0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314917 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cf00962-b6b4-5998-9975-1454f9f132c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312597}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312597} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cd890a5-ae47-5a39-8de8-b66ed079ea1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f0e13b26-bfd5-5474-9a46-4ad33e13d1cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340137" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\sec }^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{3\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{{\\tan }^{2}⁡\\theta }{3\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\right)}{3\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta } & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{1}{3{\\cos }^{4}⁡0}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta }\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{1}{3{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta } & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b10b5839-c3fe-5a3d-8320-30a9fbd4c299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313350}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313350} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd4a1e0-a3ec-521a-bf0b-3432f0cdd18b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "024f2f9f-248f-5918-9d85-569efea4127c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340150" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we can simplify the limit using trigonometric identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }{4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta } & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }÷\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}(\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the limits separately:\n\n- Consider the first limit. Notice that as $\\theta \\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$ \n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form. \n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{(\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\theta })} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the second limit. Evaluating it directly, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{4}=\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡0}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\theta \\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {lim}_{\\theta \\to 0}\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta }{4} & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314934 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1e08605-30ac-5180-b993-bb8905dcf80f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x-1}{(\\operatorname{ln}x)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313247} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0021a41-de95-5d7c-9f18-7f5e0c873094" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c69eee29-5e7a-522d-bfc9-641f3d5d4893" :question/math-academy-id "q-340341" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{x}{\\ln ⁡x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x$ and $v=\\ln ⁡x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\ln ⁡x\\cdot 1-x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x})}{{(\\ln ⁡x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\ln ⁡x-1}{{(\\ln ⁡x)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe403dd4-e8fc-5b37-a657-5cdb8ad41fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2\\operatorname{ln}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314003} :answer-field/id #uuid "392caec0-4bec-552a-a683-fe5f52bda24d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ac3d430-ffd8-5d49-8cb6-f41434a00666" :question/math-academy-id "q-340343" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{\\log }_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}\\ln ⁡3}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={\\log }_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\ln ⁡x}{\\ln ⁡3}$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\ln ⁡3}-\\frac{\\ln ⁡x}{\\ln ⁡3}\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{\\ln ⁡3}-\\frac{2x\\ln ⁡x}{\\ln ⁡3})}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\ln ⁡x}{{x}^{4}\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-2\\ln ⁡x)}{{x}^{4}\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\ln ⁡x}{{x}^{3}\\ln ⁡3} \\\\  & =(\\,1-2\\ln ⁡x\\,)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}\\ln ⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c76d532-99c8-56d3-b76b-6f4d31c9b740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312321} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9be464-5216-51f1-b7f0-351690652ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312615} {:answer/id #uuid "63e163e1-c538-5dc8-b5d6-f68763649175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312766} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1e7d60-c8b8-50db-9912-df839b7f21d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312321} :answer-field/id #uuid "1556010c-e77c-5957-9ba3-c9f9700a252b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "506088fb-b02d-55a4-8a91-d0f1c3dd9a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312626} {:answer/id #uuid "55d14253-1832-599c-aef4-8d37c05c1464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312665} {:answer/id #uuid "937c28a9-bd3f-523a-9e6d-1a75e5b0fc69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313157} :answer-field/id #uuid "1811908a-9971-5cef-9f2b-53f604906c67" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f1b5b0fe-45a4-56a8-8575-f312d7f1dcc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340368" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-9\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-0)+\\frac{1}{18}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "519814f5-ed1b-51b6-be42-701a06d5265e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312641} {:answer/id #uuid "95b4bc14-3372-51dc-8763-57839bd1c948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313172} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb0a620-4521-5768-b99c-bcb9e240ba49" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314534} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37bd8b3c-bc05-54eb-8f57-a55be5021855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312424} {:answer/id #uuid "44030d02-2f0e-52b1-b64d-de590270d455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312522} {:answer/id #uuid "d4075d04-803b-5c99-bdac-b3260aa45ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313642} {:answer/id #uuid "fa59b168-761a-5bf2-b1fd-942fcfe16bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313642} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9d5f723-31ec-5260-873d-bac32456689b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77cb7a42-6cad-573a-85fb-a6a039017afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340369" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4bab2c1-bbe5-5435-a3d2-86b96876e5b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313269} :answer-field/id #uuid "f394fa38-3822-55d6-a5b8-df60aebd964e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5544eea8-4bb4-5f9a-9cb6-d625b88a8f84" :question/math-academy-id "q-340422" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we split the integral into the sum of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(6{x}^{-1/2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2{x}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12{x}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =12({4}^{1/2}-{1}^{1/2})+\\frac{1}{3}({4}^{3}-{1}^{3}) \\\\  & =12(2-1)+\\frac{1}{3}(64-1) \\\\  & =12+21 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c51051a6-39a4-5d3d-8f84-11efa084f10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313521} :answer-field/id #uuid "618ea825-b730-590f-be99-2b4638c41dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e5793e05-ded5-5153-bb6f-74c424b138c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340441" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We split the integral into the difference of two integrals and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+2\\,\\operatorname{arccot} \\,x\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})+2(\\operatorname{arccot} (1)-\\operatorname{arccot} (0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55bf33bc-86e0-5cb8-9d56-e6d5bfb12d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312664}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312664} :answer-field/id #uuid "592e9e2a-abdb-5b4b-b338-481f7bd7b8cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd956af4-b2b4-5ae8-a37f-585154ac194f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340471" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)+\\frac{1}{10}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d8bc8a-5390-5efc-9e59-40435b355e7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313654} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71dfe58-5be5-56c6-a2e6-847bd2c5dd2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c9bb2c48-7d90-5eb1-aae2-2d07b2d4d022" :question/math-academy-id "q-340472" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{14}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e6828d-7887-5550-bbca-792a725051cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313239} :answer-field/id #uuid "82e86e27-51c1-5064-9e7c-9cbb5e3878b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f1aff49-4ed8-5efc-a461-ed82ccac840e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340474" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{20}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f042c507-b55b-5c8c-8978-63c9054e0c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313900}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313900} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e22fb33-417f-5805-ab4b-38d45822ffdf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "391451cc-b516-5347-88a9-b2eff039d5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-340487" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x+3x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right))-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{8}(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9486d2b-7ce0-5f6e-a396-c474a1abd86a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313970} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c732ec-4884-5281-a12b-632144414c6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c6c293fa-013e-59cd-b66e-3459611554eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-340489" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x+3x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddeaca9e-e824-533c-bc58-3ec2b3a38f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce118ddd-47bd-5cab-bad9-6d3152c9b1db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d43d9ab4-a760-5c76-927f-c8696da3c953" :question/math-academy-id "q-340491" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}(1-(-1))-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d074817c-9881-518c-b1ea-75ba1846c23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313605} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1a4a9f-47b6-58b2-8ec5-aab72b4b9f7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11434a0f-ad34-5cb2-96ed-81af7783f37f" :question/math-academy-id "q-340492" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc99873b-3451-5f41-ba37-844fe33217e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313989} :answer-field/id #uuid "018d0f8e-d1f2-519a-966e-53e0db26ecbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2e94a3f-da6f-5c4b-aae5-24125dac827b" :question/math-academy-id "q-340505" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-8x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+8x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{22}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(1-0)-\\frac{1}{22}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f8b6c4-dc61-5952-b970-50080ba23ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312150} {:answer/id #uuid "77366691-f549-599e-bab1-698ac3eb9e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312930} {:answer/id #uuid "cc46ef65-e857-5164-a7e5-34465ecfdb28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313571} {:answer/id #uuid "f6401f00-bf5f-521c-a263-60f469691296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313945}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313945} :answer-field/id #uuid "330a3eca-372e-59a7-9bf8-d2efffe99e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314147} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3bcbcb0-ba3d-57ed-97b9-85742d944618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413377} {:answer/id #uuid "f54e1f11-1b9d-5dcf-819b-ddcca88bb9fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413381} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d41b053-3b5f-5c34-91f8-1d643d3b5746" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413386}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0e763dfc-e659-54e6-9b52-205e19a11f13" :question/math-academy-id "q-340528" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ]$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the inner product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=1$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027eecbf-7d9e-56b4-a605-18bbb53e14b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311977} {:answer/id #uuid "4494fa18-4bec-52af-830e-3d47e3335b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312528} {:answer/id #uuid "9649e93c-dcb4-5673-af2c-491163f8d92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313174} {:answer/id #uuid "d90019b4-a024-5ea2-b7e9-d632c152b387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313682} {:answer/id #uuid "e664ec95-b5dd-5b08-ac1e-628c8f111a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313174} :answer-field/id #uuid "271f0e63-bff4-53c7-a28b-a628905d21bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084986f6-01c0-508d-b7b2-e49ecf7afbf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312035} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9be0ef-0626-58f6-8f34-f07d2341cc36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312179} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee209f2-de18-556a-b781-2693cbbb09b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312209} {:answer/id #uuid "4c486615-805d-5bc0-8681-1ee3c3e17ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312592} {:answer/id #uuid "9a09cad1-8a04-5089-8601-85ab90a53a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313194} {:answer/id #uuid "e661e1e8-358c-5402-8b71-9182bd7c2769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313799}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312035} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dd294ec-abdf-52bd-adbb-82925e22c66c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2adde187-25b8-5d4b-a204-1561619be2dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312303} {:answer/id #uuid "44b54432-6be2-557b-86a3-52063866fc0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312530} {:answer/id #uuid "91d08667-08e2-52ae-8373-62b7feb36f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313142} {:answer/id #uuid "b45c048e-2b73-557d-9a93-c538814c0f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313379} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c63573-93e1-5481-abea-0476f7532355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312530} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c5f513-5509-5ed5-bf44-fe4e4be503ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "189f6686-0cc6-5c02-8e21-1097f02fd5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-340628" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}+3$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{3}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={a}_{3}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fed8a6-dde9-59a6-bc31-184c4876910c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312040} {:answer/id #uuid "27e0630f-6397-5f50-acaf-792c1ed4a63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312279} {:answer/id #uuid "43aed6aa-dcf1-5ad7-a81d-0980770c21f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312518} {:answer/id #uuid "7f4f1e1b-19ff-53ba-846a-bf53f48da618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312998} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b040b0-f184-56be-ac1d-d88fac453333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313510} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8ab6ed-8ef1-52e1-ac24-6d360702fe0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{x}^{2}\\operator