r/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{s-11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312249} {:answer/id #uuid "4528f255-2cba-53cd-bc62-cb87b1dd8c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312534} {:answer/id #uuid "acb24084-a065-5138-8a78-fff74aac9a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s-11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313326} {:answer/id #uuid "bd474aff-4019-54e6-99f4-9a9ec582a2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11{e}^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313454} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ec69a6-e30b-535b-bd8c-ffb010c1af71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11{e}^{-s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312534} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27e109f-caec-5b21-a3bd-a64944c244ea" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314492} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102057e0-5189-5b54-8acc-a9e698e8670c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is convergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312091} {:answer/id #uuid "c46a96d5-02b8-54b6-a8d9-9864f04bee5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313518} {:answer/id #uuid "e9493367-1f37-546e-b56b-d138c349136b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313833} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf8c7d6-9857-5015-a827-3707e24520b6" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6616c7a7-a7ca-51f0-bc1c-2e3ffaffd164" :question/math-academy-id "q-340712" :question/problem "Complete the arguments in the reasoning below to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11.$First, we apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=F(s)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nWe now examine the convergence of $F(s)$ for different values of $s:$\n\n$\\,$ For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ {{field-3}}\n\n$\\,$ For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ {{field-4}}\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $L{11}=\\frac{11}{s},$ \ndefined for\n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty ),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $L{f(t)}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s\\ne 0$ or $s=0.$\n\n- If $s\\ne 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{a\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{e}^{-sa}=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & s>0, \\\\ \\mathrm{\\infty },\\hspace{1em} & s<0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =11\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s>0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is convergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s<0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }11{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\,\\text{is divergent}\n$$\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{11}{s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=11$ is $F(s)=\\frac{11}{s},$ defined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315001 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4038686e-4a45-597c-8888-fb7551c4a873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is convergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312493} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff8563d-82f7-513d-b4f0-3074c1b4c561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312857} {:answer/id #uuid "ef2d0a3d-c8f8-5eb5-90c8-8d4845ab9bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313885} :answer-field/id #uuid "177b53e3-3f02-59bc-8d8c-f14852508ef1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59582aca-2ca6-5753-8a4a-77e2ab898716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312689} {:answer/id #uuid "84d0e22f-7a7a-53b1-841c-12054df80cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "is