im_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3a/8)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3a/8))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3\\text{i}/8} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-3\\text{i}t/8}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{8}{8s+3\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4601d478-a588-5df4-bdcb-dfe347714aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312550} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e13e04-f639-5708-9462-00e0e0c7789f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-1/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313366} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b16fde-c2bf-50fa-a363-98d96160de1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>1/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313743} {:answer/id #uuid "e6069e3c-da92-5e3f-a780-b04a9c9dd854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313794} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fea87f0-0a77-526b-a0f5-91d573d623ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314138} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b2b7b3-8caa-59dd-ba04-681ce7b65e53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312114} {:answer/id #uuid "5ecbf8ab-b480-5262-aaa2-eb8439c3b483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9s+\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312732} {:answer/id #uuid "836ee434-27c2-5518-ae65-716d140b9aa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{9s-\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313032} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e81862-cb0f-5a27-8e98-3afa22e54473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9s-\\text{i}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312114} :answer-field/id #uuid "88acd212-a94d-54b5-a4f8-09cca88b08c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a11b0ae2-f252-588b-a79a-9a6f77d0e962" :question/math-academy-id "q-340750" :question/problem "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ where $t,s\\in R?$$\\,L(f(t))=$\n{{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)={e}^{-\\text{i}t/9},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-\\text{i}/9-s}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(1-\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-\\text{i}/9-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}(\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot {e}^{-\\text{i}a/9}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\n{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))].\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s<0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s<0.$\n\n- For $s=0,$ we have simply $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9),$ which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s=0.$\n\n- For $s>0,$ the term ${e}^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-a/9)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-a/9))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+\\text{i}/9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of ${e}^{-\\text{i}t/9}$ is $F(s)=\\frac{9}{9s+\\text{i}},$ defined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c197952-4742-5247-a8dd-30d9d399307e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-5s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312066} {:answer/id #uuid "692825c4-4687-5242-9fa3-044d3ff7a06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312797} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd6dff5-eff5-5a32-aac9-5e264440606c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-5s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313208} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7868f5-3770-5ddf-8f8c-b4150847c497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}({e}^{-5s}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313313}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312797} :answer-field/id #uuid "244d701e-b7f1-553c-ace0-3267b278e9b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3734f322-965c-5278-bfad-971cab7f5809" :question/math-academy-id "q-340886" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $s>0,$ where $f(t)=u(5-t)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(5-t)=\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>5\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(5-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-5s})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315033 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc2695f-0e15-5691-a028-b150d466dcf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312316} {:answer/id #uuid "725ea555-7d4c-5eaf-95eb-128a98f4ed38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312872} {:answer/id #uuid "f347fe48-c18b-5b04-9912-af36b33ea06b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313921} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8ec435-d3fb-5f32-883e-f63f94659176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312872} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0d16eb-266b-580b-b823-2ca3bad44068" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f25da9e-c33a-5999-a8c4-ac447897722c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312485} {:answer/id #uuid "525fe023-9b2c-5613-ac7a-b31e065a357b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312643} {:answer/id #uuid "b69786fc-4a61-5bdb-88d2-2cf63ec7fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313393} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe655ac-4aeb-5468-80b5-b24c11370919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312485} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece722c4-3730-5a44-bfa8-824b101a061a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f36f74d-9ded-58b3-8942-de350905b5b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-340922" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{n}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002bef8f-5454-5d6b-b487-b895705fe836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10(1-(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311961} {:answer/id #uuid "34a2bb91-7ebb-51cc-96df-55b85c36dd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(1-(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312399} {:answer/id #uuid "b22348d5-796c-5ddb-bb29-049e902078ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10(1+(-1)^{n})}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313361} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f95b12-b6ce-579c-b943-3cdb5889deae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313655} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b93e59-6c18-5ab8-b6e8-b771aa760167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313655} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c762d34-c0c7-53cc-905e-ebc3c49f9b2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a94eebf7-ea68-5ac3-9351-043b927df6ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-340926" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$\n$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-5x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{10}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{n\\pi }(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{n}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315044 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423fd3ba-62f9-5138-b68c-a795850f01c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312501} :answer-field/id #uuid "007dec11-1185-591f-a7c5-e687595c6718" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "89e6c8b8-1578-5971-8542-35407df630cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-341042" :question/problem "For the functions $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nu(4)=1,\\,{u}^{′}(4)=6,\\,v(4)=-2,\\,{v}^{′}(4)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))\n$$ at $x=4.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(x)+3{v}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=4$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}u(x)+3v(x))|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{′}(4)+3{v}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(6)+3(2) \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "697919b4-cf11-522a-a781-6ad18a82539c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312803} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd035701-4cce-591d-a7d9-3f8be8c452a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9d4d87e-59e9-54b8-822f-cb78eea065ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-341062" :question/problem "![](images/34/34966965452968c2f186efcfbec70591eded02e926eaaac8b90409381b2c8da8.png)\n\nThe area of an equilateral triangle is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the area of the equilateral triangle is increasing with respect to its side length at the moment when its side length is equal to $s=4\\,\\text{in}.$  \n\n*Hint*: The area of an equilateral triangle is $A=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}.$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change at the moment when its side length is $4$ inches is equal to\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that the area of the equilateral triangle with side $s$ is given by  \n\n$$\nA=A(s)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $A(s)$  with respect to $s$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}{s}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot (2s) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}s,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $s=4$ gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(4)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 4=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at the moment when the length of the side is $s=4\\,\\text{in}$ is $2\\sqrt{3}\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{in}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315049 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "736358be-b15d-5572-9a7d-e2c1b162c5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312889} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d51f45-9ac3-50b7-8e7e-a99791f5df84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313397} {:answer/id #uuid "bcb7f8c7-f0ee-5b82-8e68-9bf292adfc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313448} {:answer/id #uuid "ca011ffd-4317-5cd0-a523-b8d946003250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313551} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2f2e3a-830c-5c61-97de-1eeed7ae62c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313580} {:answer/id #uuid "fd385b6a-f413-51e4-8c07-5e53e44b0516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313995}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312889} :answer-field/id #uuid "c97fbae0-d928-5e16-8119-2ca0e620f0dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314569} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9aeaa1-b187-5b93-9a34-d4e755e38dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312170} {:answer/id #uuid "3de705a6-c93c-5847-aef8-686296cd036d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312475} {:answer/id #uuid "65422943-54ca-5806-b182-99db46b4b27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312773} {:answer/id #uuid "67ae037d-6d7f-5de0-9976-57829f55953f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312789} {:answer/id #uuid "c98c24e1-c972-56c4-ba20-92ddfb1aa44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312475} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddaee052-1803-54cd-a0f9-ce0838d40348" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f2c45534-de22-5957-94a0-9fb0be556b3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341107" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)=\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2 \\\\ L{{e}^{-2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+2},\\,s>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{2t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-2}+\\frac{1}{s+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+2)+(s-2)}{(s-2)(s+2)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8edf9692-6828-531e-a524-456c401eb354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313116} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f609bf-30c3-5195-9625-8bf23dbeb98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313373} {:answer/id #uuid "c61fae7a-d115-5d60-aace-221720a5ea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313528} {:answer/id #uuid "cf692c6b-eaf1-58f2-b0cd-fa16ba85d66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313596} {:answer/id #uuid "e428bf77-631f-59d6-9120-f9ce05a41509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313596} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a66528-692d-5c78-823d-ade5dfdc33d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f73f9f2-9a84-5ff9-bd27-5d1e9f589c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312738} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6fd869-adbf-5e44-889c-f05617df5aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312991} {:answer/id #uuid "830640f3-0e0d-5579-a43f-b99d0dd807e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313029} {:answer/id #uuid "a27becd5-454a-566a-b4cc-a33c60c4d7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313253} {:answer/id #uuid "a84b7be1-8f52-5f7a-a33f-707976201760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313295} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1ce046-09b5-5e82-bc8c-ef125afe175a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312991} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6cd3403-b426-5f6c-ad5f-b0f06b27b7e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25fdc2a1-548f-5db7-a267-7ea03400402a" :question/math-academy-id "q-341109" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4},\\,s>-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{1}{s+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+4)+(s-4)}{(s-4)(s+4)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-16}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (4,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-4t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-4,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(4t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d71143b0-47a4-55bc-833a-9a37cf1a0cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313670}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313670} :answer-field/id #uuid "42df9230-a42a-5643-ac02-c0cf183c1ecb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7de810d1-79ef-5695-be73-3f0627f342f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-341115" :question/problem "Calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{arcsec} ({e}^{x})$ at $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arcsec} $ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,x)=\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsec} ({e}^{x})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|{e}^{x}|\\sqrt{{({e}^{x})}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2x}-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡10) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)/2)}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c768e14f-5c12-58a7-a2f6-c76213bf259b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12s}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313533} {:answer/id #uuid "da0c792d-19bb-5761-a855-22981a61e7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313689} {:answer/id #uuid "ebfaea01-08ad-5f2d-b3c4-35c19ceb7293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313860} {:answer/id #uuid "faf65d46-a67b-54cf-9a43-1928028845c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313979} {:answer/id #uuid "ff66dd4e-ce1c-53e8-b03e-2493d92b1a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}-144}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313979} :answer-field/id #uuid "25c56102-996d-591d-9850-f3779725766d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314109} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b1e4833-125b-5275-9ddb-3e5a0c5c0ef5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312308} {:answer/id #uuid "4344e22b-2cf4-5526-888c-708789e7646f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312512} {:answer/id #uuid "4489bac6-a751-5770-af18-c4d31a4dbca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312526} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd90533-3806-5a8d-9c20-c624b5340f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313463} {:answer/id #uuid "da8bac94-2af9-5d4f-bb9e-888341b229c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313690} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5260cf-2b88-59f4-aca6-f8fbf7c13e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314004} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23a6d1f-5fa6-5ea0-84c7-199794933e97" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e8a3cfa3-5dd8-5a89-8d0e-84651dbd2d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-341127" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the sine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)=\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}-{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}-\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}{2\\text{i}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)} & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{12\\text{i}t}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}L{{e}^{-12\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}(\\frac{1}{s-12\\text{i}}-\\frac{1}{s+12\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\left(\\frac{(s+12\\text{i})-(s-12\\text{i})}{(s-12\\text{i})(s+12\\text{i})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}}\\left(\\frac{24\\text{i}}{{s}^{2}+144}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{{s}^{2}+144},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14436da6-7b8c-5459-99b4-6bfac4ea557e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312115} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9e4cdb-da70-58c7-89af-e8d816e8118a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312197} {:answer/id #uuid "6a67d383-8250-5a1a-90d2-d860785ccd1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312811} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a5bb4c-e052-588c-8333-00b1ec43ae2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313742} {:answer/id #uuid "f4191730-dc53-522b-bd9c-0a28f4609b05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}-25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313742} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd9d091-035d-5977-ba5e-edd708d5f7ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314383} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f877140-44e1-512e-aa01-58a47c03d18a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312212} {:answer/id #uuid "5787223e-84df-5b67-8318-493beb86482a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312680} {:answer/id #uuid "5d707f3b-b3d7-52ed-8c43-aff476c90d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s>5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312722} {:answer/id #uuid "6f0cd83e-16f9-5e10-8198-cb4268d7ac22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312849} {:answer/id #uuid "c190ebbe-c34f-57a2-84bb-70203879f609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313489} {:answer/id #uuid "d70e6448-340a-5c5d-bb7d-9a3c9421077e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\ge 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312680} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d45f06-6418-52b0-822f-68bc8cdb0326" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c15c9946-0f44-5115-b665-0c4efd18e05a" :question/math-academy-id "q-341137" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)=\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{5\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-5\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-5\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+5\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{(s+5\\text{i})+(s-5\\text{i})}{(s-5\\text{i})(s+5\\text{i})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+25}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+25},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2d52500-a992-5060-8249-a0e5114ed838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2(1+x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313765} :answer-field/id #uuid "63e24a99-cc76-5643-98c9-e83dbb7d91d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0bdae838-0b95-584e-856d-e0740682daed" :question/math-academy-id "q-341145" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arccot} \\,(\\sqrt{x}).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of $\\operatorname{arccot} $ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccot} \\,x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule and the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccot} \\,(\\sqrt{x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})+\\sqrt{x}\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{1+{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1+x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccot} (\\sqrt{x})}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{1}{2(1+x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa4792b-5b15-527a-992e-0d96170ab1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450530} {:answer/id #uuid "f9461d03-b412-574d-9841-bce08dceb69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450531} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1e573c-09ca-594f-a9ba-3b2660311b38" :answer-field/key "$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d83cac8f-f51f-527a-b720-44ad32045870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450532} {:answer/id #uuid "333f0aac-7943-517c-87c6-fb5685a1c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450533}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450532} :answer-field/id #uuid "611505c9-97d8-564c-8e0d-e557fa6b701e" :answer-field/key "$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e9805a-d060-5fce-8210-0537d29aaf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450534} {:answer/id #uuid "7605eb1c-ab43-5593-8185-dd779575eaf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450535} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ba82cd-be54-5931-a8f3-844d7c5db9bc" :answer-field/key "$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ea143c38-1de5-5608-b272-c1150223e597" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”; “$f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b6039-6dba-5338-ad23-1478945f062c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312315} {:answer/id #uuid "9f284ed1-4066-5e95-87bb-5b55ec0f8548" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313228} {:answer/id #uuid "c2493663-5905-5150-a908-10d6ccd3070d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313496} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5f4a32-8e0e-5318-a0fb-d3ef6b154d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313595} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbf2c5e-c13f-5b6e-8cda-4efcf6a8da52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{20(1+(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313857}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313496} :answer-field/id #uuid "08fb48cd-85db-5f6d-a1af-605e6f21bd8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbd91c18-5323-50d3-919d-9acc9d2aec83" :question/math-academy-id "q-341259" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=10x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{n}.$$\\,{a}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}\nfor $n\\ge 1$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=10x$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{20}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{20}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{n\\pi }((\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{20(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{20((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315075 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "292d09e2-366d-50c7-86f4-6898667fcfec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312287} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce91827-52f2-597b-9389-99aefab23d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312467} {:answer/id #uuid "d2f3ea3e-9ae5-5738-ad8d-11b7d1754191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313629} {:answer/id #uuid "fac9f330-237b-5b89-aeee-ab1656cc9f15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313978} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a80956-9df8-56b2-a0d0-12c93245bdf9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "527f0bcc-5a93-5e85-bb82-ec6249af1987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312645} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bf166b-a34f-50d0-b8d4-974fcf1f2890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313307} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b0205f-4c60-524e-8704-c01df057b15d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313371} {:answer/id #uuid "f20e3a40-f5e3-59d1-9c69-7ef206e5ab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313915} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea74207b-3afc-53cf-9987-8304a8d0ebf5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3e04b614-2191-5d74-a0de-542e6efc3422" :question/math-academy-id "q-341265" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=5x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(2+\\frac{5}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{5}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((5\\pi +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{5}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{5}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{10(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(2+\\frac{5}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{10((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dfdcad1-3216-541c-b8d2-77d8409211b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312076} {:answer/id #uuid "189ef403-3001-55d4-809e-ce42b49b7898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312148} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3a134f-8c80-5edc-b450-8ef896ef6adb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312176} {:answer/id #uuid "d43cbe44-1488-5d05-8469-4706ed848443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313647}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba1c8d1-0126-51d9-94e5-7f4780b0e204" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48e82861-42e7-58fa-be43-4e44d4a4bc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312561} {:answer/id #uuid "66357736-37bd-59f8-92ef-23c688be02fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312779} {:answer/id #uuid "89a8b5f7-be05-5867-84f9-ef9e118244a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313069} {:answer/id #uuid "c286d299-5d5d-5d61-a3a0-9feaf7fcbba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312561} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92f1bcd-414a-5ea0-b35e-ecb516aedf69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dcb7f9a-5c58-5bc4-a651-bf664e0c0d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-341266" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x+4$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(x+4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+4\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(x+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{1}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((\\pi +4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)-(0+4)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx))+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{1}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(4+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb713a9-182a-58c5-8920-2a63b83c7f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312191} {:answer/id #uuid "240146db-8d91-59be-b64b-76e1c8746ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312243} {:answer/id #uuid "fd180a8b-f784-5dc2-b774-7210e71ce47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313993} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0b1b70-4c58-554b-a64c-113f6eb2a24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312191} :answer-field/id #uuid "269a3def-e5fb-5139-bdd6-b154cb225918" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00fa78b3-f990-59e4-9c5d-d0332b6aff1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311967} {:answer/id #uuid "388c3d71-2ecb-56b2-a8f8-8278a8c14822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312433} {:answer/id #uuid "e4bb1a08-e278-5d30-b8cb-9166ede28c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313781} {:answer/id #uuid "e8bb46c1-cb30-5e51-80bc-28b9893d881b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312433} :answer-field/id #uuid "878c7582-0be7-5a08-9eeb-a39c44e09857" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7d424ca-a5eb-5340-a732-023666fa3414" :question/math-academy-id "q-341267" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3x+1$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((3\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(1+\\frac{3}{2}\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f280615-7fd5-5cf2-a85d-227ba49cab92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312351} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d6609d-2d93-58b4-9f9f-bd7af039aa99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{\\pi {n}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313828} {:answer/id #uuid "ef42de0d-7275-58c7-9850-c9f45558f11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313888} {:answer/id #uuid "f1259b66-32a0-5323-81d7-5348f6ac97bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312351} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d94c0e-afc0-5b2f-a55b-93f6395b9993" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28794a0a-b05e-53bb-9413-8ed1528f3c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312283} {:answer/id #uuid "542eacb9-7165-5039-bebb-8327f3afbc2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312655} {:answer/id #uuid "663883ce-10e1-5f1f-9ed6-0d0656516e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312780} {:answer/id #uuid "d22a4c59-199a-5686-bc6e-f03b75417845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313619}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312283} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4b31acd-8857-5337-bb14-dbffd8c9fdf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c2ef579-bdc5-59bc-8cd2-73be3fa9875e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341269" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-2x+5$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(5-\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-2x+5$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2x+5)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-2x+5\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-2x+5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-2\\pi +5)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(5-\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19318467-73e5-540d-b86c-017adc969dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\left(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312153} {:answer/id #uuid "07aa64d0-a645-5949-a1b8-1717374a9409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2(\\frac{5\\pi^{2}}{3}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549325} :answer-field/id #uuid "709ccfa5-c570-5e8b-84e0-9153e5971d2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c31042af-d0b1-56e0-8fc6-7b7b6e402e06" :question/math-academy-id "q-341276" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(5{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315080 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27402392-ef7b-543e-a95c-51569128931d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312273} {:answer/id #uuid "b2388473-f7b4-5371-bdaf-2f5d22ad6092" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(\\sqrt{2}-2)}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513999} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63f2945-3857-5e6d-882c-11e5148b361f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "86b3942b-d5fe-58b1-8280-9f2a9124377a" :question/math-academy-id "q-341283" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a30cec-1c74-58b6-a352-b55dbaad5ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312860} {:answer/id #uuid "6291bc20-b33b-51a2-81f3-8c4774fb834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420177} :answer-field/id #uuid "850f065e-3ad8-58f4-8d41-45b829bfc32b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1a98a94-1685-5290-8055-415073e9036d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341285" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-6) \\\\  & =\\frac{6(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067f1f01-946a-508e-8cd3-212922a30cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312018} {:answer/id #uuid "1c9ff7e5-36fe-5003-9799-45faaef030fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312189} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6042f8-3d6d-5360-9b00-ffce5586d196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312954} {:answer/id #uuid "9d043cac-3ffa-5f03-90e2-e0c96f5392e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313218} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d8c3cb-4ca0-5236-8d59-7a3088019974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313774}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313218} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c8bc7b-4cd5-5227-9be1-4358ff24b62e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ffdb817-1342-5108-9142-29c41b65bfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312744} {:answer/id #uuid "65761c3a-2090-56c5-acc5-a7f5ae659917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312774} {:answer/id #uuid "821891c1-b858-5bda-9626-34240d7900c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313023} {:answer/id #uuid "832cde4d-8aee-5316-8503-fd0c2df797ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313031} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ae89cc-5e23-5a8d-aa64-bd8dbd7a3313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313912} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f86a36-0a17-52e0-b0d0-d33c3e50f91e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314358} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec31f7d-80af-523d-b38f-294b8ad028bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨1,1⟩}{⟨f(x),1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312081} {:answer/id #uuid "0f14e142-4150-51d0-8ede-ebf4f1bd28f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312085} {:answer/id #uuid "114b3cdc-ae09-538f-94d9-f5c3e4392e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312098} {:answer/id #uuid "992303bc-e624-5324-b23d-d78df9b645e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313189} {:answer/id #uuid "dd23267e-19c8-5ea7-b897-d3089e3d52ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312098} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0b3226d-31b8-5280-8683-73d653641be2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a5448f9e-c9df-59b5-abdc-33e558c56456" :question/math-academy-id "q-341309" :question/problem "Let $F(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{0}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that's piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),1⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩ \\\\ &= \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),1⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1,1⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),1⟩ & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{2⟨f(x),1⟩}{⟨1,1⟩} \\\\ {a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d2b4d4-59ed-5f0d-9922-89c02bc8942c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi +2)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312144} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7dfd21-ea92-5be3-8cba-ebde5cac0a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312186} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ef0a66-4c64-5da9-8e07-1ee81ddb44b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313358} {:answer/id #uuid "e47a9901-72d0-5e49-98e9-0f5ae120d820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312186} :answer-field/id #uuid "27702b14-e617-57ac-90a9-1bb0c61d35a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6441ae36-adf1-5855-b981-8fc098d80d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312770} {:answer/id #uuid "7389b0c8-c427-5783-917f-54f1d71de35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312890} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f60e40-1d50-5fed-9993-320e426adf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313640} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0e894e-bcb8-5db4-8c39-fd7a667523d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312890} :answer-field/id #uuid "84d7a55f-6b4e-5c7e-90c0-f352d65aa8ea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ac9732d3-1086-5e0e-8f66-b1641de82b66" :question/math-academy-id "q-341336" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-3x+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-3x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{3}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{3}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2+\\frac{3}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-3\\pi +2)(-1)^{n}-2)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2).\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier sine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(3\\pi -2)+2)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a485b2b2-9921-5fd5-bd9d-f8899940f0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313268} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2136e8-d9d6-5af6-a549-5e509b077f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "are not orthogonal" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313579}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313268} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a717f2-4ea4-5657-bb93-c452e09b74ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14b12c1a-93ae-5bc6-9fe8-42c8eda21ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312119} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4f5c80-0a03-57e7-8507-5198d48c6bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312177} {:answer/id #uuid "4693d13b-e7f3-533f-a819-ee280e597461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7359a2-3aa5-59d3-a824-65f7cf6dc11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312119} :answer-field/id #uuid "98de6f2f-ef74-5be0-a949-95ef2c54203f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22b987d7-9005-5317-ab10-48027150c40e" :question/math-academy-id "q-341340" :question/problem "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "We begin by using the product-to-sum identity\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{14}(1-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)$ $\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315087 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ca1466-3eec-5fa9-bed6-dcfed93e4962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312519} {:answer/id #uuid "60c066e8-4e00-5c0e-bda1-b64886fce363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6(1-(-1)^{n})}{\\pi n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312749} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ee5da4-98b9-5572-a02f-cac3b061b79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(-2\\pi -3)-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313283} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fd3b91-82ee-5393-9657-edcaf670b225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi +3)-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312519} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f5ee118-4881-5835-9bc6-98f88b571da8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "44334d32-3a61-5824-a7c7-cbdf4232a9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341381" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier sine series of the **odd** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${b}_{n}.$$\\,{b}_{n}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For our Fourier sine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(-\\frac{1}{n}[(-2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-2\\pi +3)(-1)^{n}-3)=\\frac{2}{n\\pi }((-1)^{n}(2\\pi -3)+3).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4888f926-ac9b-5bbc-8435-71b677b35500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}x}{4}+{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312559} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b7e347-dabe-5d35-a8f1-93d368fa3fc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "30336cd1-38ee-5642-8e7c-8269174ccf53" :question/math-academy-id "q-341383" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{5\\sin ⁡x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sin ⁡x)=\\cos ⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5\\sin ⁡x}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(\\cos ⁡x)+\\frac{1}{4}(4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cos ⁡x}{4}+{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315092 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036dfb6e-d5e6-5be1-8ad1-c65e7972833c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-8s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311989} {:answer/id #uuid "5f54360a-39eb-5185-a1f9-18863dd337be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}({e}^{-8s}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312736} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15f614-f94e-57ca-b9bf-3efb838ad3dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}(1+{e}^{-8s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312956} {:answer/id #uuid "e8183391-fec1-500b-9633-7caf1b627425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-8s})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313817} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a9d19fc-cfe5-519b-b802-0c86f16c315a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb684e6a-ec18-594d-bf56-9e167c4180ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-341398" :question/problem "Use the definition of Laplace transforms to calculate $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ for $s>0,$ where $f(t)=u(8-t)$ and $u$ is the unit step function.$\\,F(s)=L{f(t)}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the unit step function is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t)=\\begin{cases}0,\\hspace{1em} & t<0 \\\\ 1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for the piecewise function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(8-t)=\\begin{cases}1,\\hspace{1em} & t\\le 8 \\\\ 0,\\hspace{1em} & t>8\\end{cases}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }u(8-t){e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}1\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }0\\cdot {e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute this integral for $s>0$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}{e}^{-st}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{e}^{-st}}{s}]}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-8s}}{s}+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}(1-{e}^{-8s})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fec0518-6db1-5a1a-87fd-0be56c14ec24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312357} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac09dbc9-4c7f-5daf-a0d0-d838c2f1ffb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e50cad91-21d9-539e-b085-381c16d9469f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341492" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(1.5,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<2)=0.5910,$ use the table value to find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<2)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le 2)=0.5910\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.5910 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-1.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5910 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.5910 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.23)=0.5910\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.5910)=0.23.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.23 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.23} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.173\\,91,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(2.173\\,91)^{2}≈4.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b99e4117-0aad-50da-a0ea-7f1605f6f881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4+10{y}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313421}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186313421} :answer-field/id #uuid "a13b0fcc-1e2e-571c-b6df-a879bcbc6ca8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aed30416-75f3-525a-aed4-35612b1daf20" :question/math-academy-id "q-341608" :question/problem "Given that $x=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+5{y}^{2},$ express $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $y.$\n\n<div class=\"question-equation fraction-equation\">\n<span>$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"answer-fraction\">\n<span class=\"answer-fraction-numerator\">${y}^{3}$</span>\n<span class=\"answer-fraction-denominator\">{{field-1}}</span>\n</span>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}+5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-2{y}^{-2}+5{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2})+5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2(-2{y}^{-3})+5(2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{y}^{3}}+10y \\\\  & =\\frac{4+10{y}^{4}}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4+10{y}^{4}}.\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "02b9c814-f68f-51e2-997f-6e7210f4ec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315132} {:answer/id #uuid "1a84d9d0-34f1-5070-ab3a-7f7e403badd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315273} {:answer/id #uuid "22bfb74d-7466-514f-8783-d9caa64c1914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3L}{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315310} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2d45f1-47c9-5d69-9fc5-5f9056932802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315381} {:answer/id #uuid "52efc260-d6a0-58d6-b571-9bf310f2d9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315592} {:answer/id #uuid "52f0d164-9fa6-5d18-bff3-6e9d4751f959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315593} {:answer/id #uuid "5586e6bd-914b-5563-88d9-3ff9d4e94fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315609} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbd3f96-5bd1-5a55-90b7-77dcd30d989b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315639} {:answer/id #uuid "6d76e4fd-89fd-54ff-ac6c-8bb15638346c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315738} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9a1e6c-342c-5c39-8d21-957ba06f386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315809} {:answer/id #uuid "7f086ea4-301a-5951-9b24-f053bd0e4029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2L}{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315817} {:answer/id #uuid "88de033c-de61-572b-a229-6c9087c62a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315883} {:answer/id #uuid "9a182076-bba9-533d-9110-45fd7a130e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315981} {:answer/id #uuid "a92605a9-32a7-57bd-9a3b-b250bbf4443e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316069} {:answer/id #uuid "aa69c24b-94b2-5654-91a1-b9c851a3df8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316077} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfe3074-e744-5103-acc4-36c41d19b6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3L}{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316273} {:answer/id #uuid "e39a3f48-7f97-55da-b06f-2dd831b7858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316378} {:answer/id #uuid "e8932200-fa03-5233-bf3d-aa0ad11900c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4435a70a-77cc-5e7e-b048-7c4b76582587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315517} {:answer/id #uuid "8b283206-1b95-587f-b532-66683c178c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315894} {:answer/id #uuid "975d1fa6-b591-54c4-90cf-6a9529e17910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315960} {:answer/id #uuid "ad61ced4-ec55-5441-a9a9-45c801eeb706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316095} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c76a0e-922a-59b6-91d8-dc7d39bab4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316464}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316464} :answer-field/id #uuid "935938ad-81de-54c6-8470-5aaf4677dc78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d44a24d-4785-56ce-9a93-d0fdc876c058" :question/math-academy-id "q-341641" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u(x-1)+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{3})+⋯,\\,g(x)=u(x+4)+u(x-6),\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ 1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,1].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $x=1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{3},…,$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=-4$ and $x=6,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{-}}g(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to -{4}^{+}}g(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{-}}g(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {6}^{+}}g(x)=2\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [-6,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[-6,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/9e/9e447c0672628ce8b304726f36885f18661bbba790ae34ca806aaae95a465bbb.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3af800bc-5c11-5056-842d-70945061727a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315458} {:answer/id #uuid "6b472db5-3df6-5b0e-bee7-65a8c940955b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315720} {:answer/id #uuid "7365f17c-666d-5cf0-9616-4b33bccec490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315774} {:answer/id #uuid "876c05e6-9f14-5b27-862f-4048bf36c855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315877} {:answer/id #uuid "d766206d-39fa-5866-a74f-51af98d35a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f,g$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316320}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315458} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c1deb93-53a0-5e42-8cd5-cda2ad39c103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b75ee8-7527-5091-aa36-59d8ffc333e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341645" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & ⌈x⌉\\text{ is odd}\\end{aligned},\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $⌈x⌉$ is the ceiling function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,…,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]⊂[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](images/c9/c93cb4cc987ec951236c40a6e961a9eb9f180a594128d4b871692a140faa31e6.png)\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty \n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95ff5c56-c73a-5a77-bdee-f837d2bc21b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315950} {:answer/id #uuid "993fd468-56fe-585f-a0f0-ddb03100c76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315974} {:answer/id #uuid "cec9548e-2fc8-5712-905c-2bfc249d4a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316285} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ce13a7-1032-5911-b9b8-3007a371c8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316496} {:answer/id #uuid "ffde2f0e-d4a7-59f2-a3da-c7b1174fd7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315950} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab034a36-e7f0-5427-b0a4-f6f717e2e3b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9ee18f19-a1e6-5a78-a0e0-7894f5c741d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341646" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{2})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{4})+u\\,(x-\\frac{1}{8})+⋯,\\,g(x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,\\,h(x)=u(x-2)+u(x-5)\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $I⊆R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]⊂I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Notice that the function has jump discontinuities at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{1}{16},…,\n$$ so it has infinitely many discontinuities on this closed interval.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are infinite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{-}}g(x)=+\\infty ,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /{2}^{+}}g(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ since it is continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ except at the points $x=2$ and $x=5,$ where the one-sided limits exist and are finite:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}h(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}h(x)=1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{-}}h(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to {5}^{+}}h(x)=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7ba7c05-abae-54c2-8308-798505248b1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316247}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316247} :answer-field/id #uuid "b87f1c6e-c8d2-5b9d-b0ea-9f20d81972ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9f19fd2-6f5a-5fbb-8759-a811885f9345" :question/math-academy-id "q-341651" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-1,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X>1)=0.0618,$ use the table to find $\\sigma ,$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9382\\,$ | $\\,0.9394\\,$ | $\\,0.9406\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9495\\,$ | $\\,0.9505\\,$ | $\\,0.9515\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =1-P(X>1) \\\\  & =1-0.0618 \\\\  & =0.9382\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.9382 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-(-1)}{\\sigma }) & =0.9382 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.9382 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1.54)=0.9382\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.9382)=1.54.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =1.54 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1.54} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈1.30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a8e441-b6b2-591b-944b-3cfddb0ac398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315690} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2534596-9656-56a2-a31c-8adf543ca897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a054739-7d5f-59e4-b190-1f466818f897" :question/math-academy-id "q-341655" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 1)=0.8413,$ use the table to find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04625262-02e3-5735-baa9-8341f10fe02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315140} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfdc307-43ba-59d0-bfef-8dec92c5a903" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a20dd68-509a-5704-9c10-a08b87646850" :question/math-academy-id "q-341745" :question/problem "If $x={y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(4,-1).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4}-\\frac{3}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{4})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-3(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+3{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}+\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{5}+3}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4{y}^{5}+3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(4,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,-1)} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{4(-1)^{5}+3}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bc8dbd-b2e6-5cdf-a87f-15c55de69984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316317} :answer-field/id #uuid "150499da-4d4c-5a7a-a641-6e789fc3eda1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cedc590a-5ca0-5fc5-bd76-db896782c449" :question/math-academy-id "q-341751" :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13,$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(5,8).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{16}{\\sqrt[3]{y}}+13) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-16{y}^{-1/3}+13) \\\\  & =-16\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(13) \\\\  & =-16(-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{-4/3})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{-4/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{4}}{16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(5,8),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(5,8)} & =\\frac{3{(\\sqrt[3]{8})}^{4}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(16)}{16} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5940053-6ffd-56dc-91ae-1c3e0c96776a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316147} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa18cac4-b60b-5b97-ba75-3318b18e72b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3fca9bbb-c820-579a-91ba-addcd4437f36" :question/math-academy-id "q-341757" :question/problem "If $x=-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(-14,1).$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{y}}-2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2y\\cdot {y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-12{y}^{-1/2}-2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1/2})-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-12\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){y}^{-3/2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =6{y}^{-3/2}-3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{y\\sqrt{y}}-3\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-3{y}^{2}}{y\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{y\\sqrt{y}}{6-3{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(-14,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-14,1)} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{1}}{6-3(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51741981-d651-523a-97ac-5191c30e9015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315583} {:answer/id #uuid "6d76e4fd-89fd-54ff-ac6c-8bb15638346c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921