8a5c-8ff6ca02f537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316153} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfe3074-e744-5103-acc4-36c41d19b6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3L}{\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316077} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88b9863-4e5d-5398-aae2-2b7abfa62918" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bc07baec-9d65-5776-a893-8007dc5102e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-341781" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2L$-periodic extension of a function $F(x)$ from $x\\in [-L,L)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({{field-1}})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({{field-2}}).\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd sine coefficient of the series is\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $F(x)={x}^{5}$ and $L=4,$ the $3$rd sine coefficient becomes\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}={{field-4}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that multiplying the argument of a function by $k\\ne 0$ represents the horizontal stretching of the corresponding graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{k}.$\n\nNow, let $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x.$ Then, since $f(x)$ has a period of $2L,$ the function\n\n$$\ng(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\n$$\n\nmust have a period of $2L\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}=2\\pi .$ \n\nThe Fourier series of $g(s)$ is\n\n$$\ng(s)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns),\n$$\n\nwhere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ {a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1 \\\\ {b}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(ns)\\,\\text{d}s,\\,n\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $s=\\frac{\\pi }{L}x$ and using the fact that $g(s)=f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s),$ we get the Fourier series of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3$rd sine coefficient of $f(x)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }g(s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }s)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(\\frac{L}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}(\\frac{\\pi }{L}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $L=4$ and $f(x)={x}^{5},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {\\int }_{-4}^{4}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "264561c5-9799-51c9-a009-d1202a71c2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315334} :answer-field/id #uuid "79eafde2-60a3-502c-bcb3-a8d0d2d51c6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e12f9894-53cb-5435-b393-ea7e08594626" :question/math-academy-id "q-341789" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ The Fourier series of the $2$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and $f(x)=6{x}^{2}+5x+1$ for $x\\in [-1,1).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+5x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(2+\\frac{5}{2}+1)-((-2)+\\frac{5}{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c05872fd-2a9b-58df-9691-c08281965b4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316203} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc43cd4e-b215-5cd4-800d-581dd2d98912" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6969b41a-9a37-587e-8acc-9fdaedc3d0de" :question/math-academy-id "q-341790" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}]}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(({4}^{3}-{4}^{2})-((-4)^{3}-(-4)^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(48-(-80)) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "683d00f4-ccba-5afe-b662-8d0eabf9f77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315704}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315704} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a2d7910-bf07-556d-9ad6-5470d09fa25a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4ff4add4-78d2-5a7b-99c9-19043e538182" :question/math-academy-id "q-341791" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{5}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-4$ for $x\\in [-5,5).$ The Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4$ for $x\\in [-5,5).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}(3{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[{x}^{3}-4x]}_{-5}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(({5}^{3}-4\\cdot 5)-((-5)^{3}-4\\cdot (-5))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(105-(-105)) \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ef1fedc-4ece-5dbd-a34d-b1853ff20c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315653}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315653} :answer-field/id #uuid "c46ee55a-7e04-50e3-aea9-605338de37eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "648d398e-a28b-5562-9aa7-9703b45cf311" :question/math-academy-id "q-341793" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{7}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\in [-7,7).$ The Fourier series of the $14$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=7$ and $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\in [-7,7).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{7}{\\int }_{-7}^{7}(3{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}{[{x}^{3}-x]}_{-7}^{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(({7}^{3}-7)-((-7)^{3}-(-7))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(336-(-336)) \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1187d685-6f3a-5f79-ba01-9ff1d5115820" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315224} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4408d37-6153-5bfe-9cc4-a50aed7144fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f681e236-f02a-536f-b3c2-833df650a871" :question/math-academy-id "q-341805" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{8}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ The Fourier series of the $16$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=8$ and $f(x)=-x+5$ for $x\\in [-8,8).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-8}^{8}(-x+5)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+5x]}_{-8}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}((-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}+5\\cdot 8)-(-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-8)^{2}+5\\cdot (-8))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(8-(-72)) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa539445-6253-5223-821b-24bdb969bfe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{40}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316518} :answer-field/id #uuid "077e7228-e35c-5575-8a2a-4c6370e4885f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "89318c25-1cdb-5fb3-8e2f-8d5f3a050ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341833" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{4}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${b}_{3}.$\n\n${b}_{3}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the sine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and $f(x)=-5x+2$ for $x\\in [-4,4).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{b}_{3}=\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-5x+2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-5x+2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{4}(uv\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x))(-5)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }{[(-5x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)]}_{-4}^{4}-\\frac{20}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }((-18)(-1)-(22)(-1))-\\frac{80}{9{\\pi }^{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\cdot 40) \\\\  & =-\\frac{40}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfdc63a9-81e1-5712-aacb-47ce364408eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316359} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffea591d-4a5e-53f6-9c48-f7ec134caf67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e12d099b-7b80-5e58-a8d0-f694cba87a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-341870" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n}{3}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $6\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the exact value of ${a}_{4}.$\n\n${a}_{4}=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For the Fourier series of a $2L$-periodic function, the cosine coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{L}{\\int }_{-L}^{L}f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3\\pi $ and $f(x)=-3x+1$ for $x\\in [-3\\pi ,3\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3\\pi }x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-3x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-3x+1\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-3 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\frac{3}{4}{[(-3x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }-{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }(\\frac{3}{4}[(-9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )-(9\\pi +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\pi )]+\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0+\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right){[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}x)]}_{-3\\pi }^{3\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-\\frac{27}{16}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\pi )]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{27}{16\\pi }(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394dcb33-d062-5610-9413-8738ad02131a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315445} :answer-field/id #uuid "a39cc58f-f168-54d3-ba4a-afd37f6750ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5e18cf65-d9e0-507c-bafc-1a7de4ad3567" :question/math-academy-id "q-341981" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}x-5, & x<2, \\\\ 1, & x\\ge 2.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x-5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-5x){|}_{-4}^{2}\\,+\\,x{|}_{2}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}-5(2)\\,-\\,(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}-5(-4))]\\,+\\,[\\,6-2\\,] \\\\  & =[1-10\\,-\\,(4+20)]+4 \\\\  & =[-9-24]+4 \\\\  & =-33+4 \\\\  & =-29.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317190 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80592157-1fdb-5e01-851d-d4d9a2def852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315824} :answer-field/id #uuid "86d59564-fbce-500f-9999-d29e15a7f0fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "171aa441-28c9-56ed-921a-96a0833a165c" :question/math-academy-id "q-341991" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-2<X<2)=0.6578,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2}$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8264\\,$ | $\\,0.8289\\,$ | $\\,0.8315\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ | $\\,0.8554\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ 2P(0<X<2) & =0.6578 \\\\ P(0<X<2) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-P(X<0) & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2)-0.5 & =0.3289 \\\\ P(X<2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =0.8289 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{2-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.8289 \\\\ Φ\\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.8289 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.95)=0.8289\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.8289)=0.95.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.95 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.95} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈2.105\\,26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =(2.105\\,26)^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62132428-191b-5bdb-8047-c131604498ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315668}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315668} :answer-field/id #uuid "686733a1-a011-561c-9118-65ee67b8331e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e084cbe-6414-54d1-af07-4d96798fc98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342004" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}-x+4, & x<1, \\\\ 2x-3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2x-3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+4x){|}_{-3}^{1}+({x}^{2}-3x){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\,-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+4\\cdot 1-(-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{2}+4(-3))]+\\,[{4}^{2}-3\\cdot 4-({1}^{2}-3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2}+4-(-\\frac{9}{2}-12)]+[16-12-(1-3)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{7}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{33}{2}\\right)]+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2}+6 \\\\  & =20+6 \\\\  & =26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b7fc5e2-407b-5358-a7a8-2ada9887d3a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315803} :answer-field/id #uuid "df961fbc-ba14-55ec-8ae8-680dec46dda4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0a5904e5-ff9f-50d9-91cb-c697a66fa301" :question/math-academy-id "q-342008" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}(\\frac{1}{2}x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4))]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a34cf8c-f9f9-5082-8ee4-5a43a6a7eef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315543} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc25a7bd-5f8b-5142-bd15-2ca2aaac680c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c8d2b0d-3143-5cce-8ab8-84982116a634" :question/math-academy-id "q-342011" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{cases}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318eb2a8-8c99-528a-b650-3ef675903063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315404} {:answer/id #uuid "80bd5ea6-072a-4a21-89e8-0504bcadbb83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+12}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186725520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315404} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d4f768-7f1c-5457-b769-1e7a1c0d47c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "36c42cd0-1bd6-5da5-bcdd-62163c6c7278" :question/math-academy-id "q-342049" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=2y-\\frac{6}{y},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-\\frac{6}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2y-6{y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2(1)-6(-{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2+6{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{6}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}+6}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(4,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(4,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2(3)^{2}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f682182f-4187-5c58-b891-e436d925ac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{11}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316494}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316494} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8095d7c-7888-58df-bb91-75240c8b1c76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "97a8acd9-41d2-5ef2-85c4-307d0b8faeeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342050" :question/problem "Given a curve $x=-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2},$ what is the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)?$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-\\frac{5}{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-5{y}^{-1}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-5(-{y}^{-2})+2y \\\\  & =2y+5{y}^{-2} \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{3}+5}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2{y}^{3}+5}.\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(-4,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2(1)^{3}+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (x-(-4)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{4}{7}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{7}x+\\frac{11}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27006cbc-09e8-5b56-9df1-8274709af358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315339} :answer-field/id #uuid "53c19706-9609-54a6-857b-4d99fd74f3fa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "797b642c-be56-5ed2-9827-fad42ffdc274" :question/math-academy-id "q-342070" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(2<X<6)=0.2812,$ find ${\\sigma }^{2},$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $2<X<6$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu =4.$*\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ 2P(4<X<6) & =0.2812 \\\\ P(4<X<6) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-P(X<4) & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6)-0.5 & =0.1406 \\\\ P(X<6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =0.6406 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6-4}{\\sigma }) & =0.6406 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.6406 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.36)=0.6406\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6406)=0.36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =0.36 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{0.36} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈5.555\\,56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}=(5.555\\,56)^{2}≈30.86\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ba581c-ebc6-55ea-99ee-e980b997d941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315698}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315698} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea3ff45-b371-5b5e-8ab3-a1c221cf4178" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2cc6d36c-e26a-5fcd-bcb0-d88e0fe39c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-342153" :question/problem "Find the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line to the curve $y=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2).$\n\n${m}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{4},2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3}}}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope ${m}^{′}$ of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{m} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14739741-a199-5044-95db-68e615cfe350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315242} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c1804f-c3c3-527f-ad52-389c7f415fc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316736} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef369bbf-3dd9-544a-b45c-a35adc1c8bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316448} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5cbe1d4-2ba5-517a-85ba-01b462393fb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "225f0ba1-57bb-5f34-91e4-9ca304d64d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342168" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}$ at the point $(2,-6).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3}.\n$$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{3})=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}-\\frac{21}{4}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,-6):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}({2}^{5})-\\frac{21}{4}({2}^{2})=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ -3(y+6) & =x-2 \\\\ -3y-18 & =x-2 \\\\ x+3\\cdot y & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eaf6866-59c7-5bcf-85b0-5e039d9cf80c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315568} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee849c8-86a4-5fad-acfb-56e96048a591" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "901ca621-ed5f-5750-a913-063b18972900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315924} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a60e86-2a15-5a46-a72a-b8277699810a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "773759d8-9f76-5cef-8ffc-9fa90fd16743" :question/math-academy-id "q-342190" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}).$\n\n$8x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt[3]{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(\\frac{1}{8},\\frac{3}{4}):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)}^{2}}}=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-\\frac{3}{4} & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16(y-\\frac{3}{4}) & =16\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8(x-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\ 16y-12 & =-8x+1 \\\\ 8x+16\\cdot y & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ce1afa-8b88-5a8a-8cdc-4e464a1bb102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315162} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad31bfe-fcf5-5162-a8e6-4281f1f124b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "28f921c8-6fd7-5d79-b2ff-0d1df567197f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342215" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c403f11093b76facafbecb72bcc413640ac67fe6ed73d6b64374230f39e29aba.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the left-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nThe general equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ and $y=-1$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2} & =4p(-3) \\\\ 1 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70ae7c16-f617-5a07-9a94-06290a107038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315753} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8fd179b-7b70-514b-b12d-26baef9b2e24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4233b404-fc80-58bf-a431-9b66af37156a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342231" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1/3)}+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1+9}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d997c12d-a03b-5296-a830-6db4be672df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316331}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316331} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f9fad05-4c08-5c4b-bf58-5859bb52824f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45bf2a7e-b636-5c97-90c2-25563ca38da6" :question/math-academy-id "q-342311" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ for the curve $2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}=2.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-{x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+[2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}]-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}-2x{y}^{2}}{6{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}y}\\right)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-2(1)(1)^{2}}{6(1)^{2}+2(1)^{2}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c48093-236c-529a-a8a5-bc47c821574a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315450} :answer-field/id #uuid "0833a293-5ce1-5cef-ab4d-f3003c06003a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316569} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d876ff73-6c82-5b9c-ae49-36253a7b0032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316325} :answer-field/id #uuid "92c6806c-bbda-5418-b219-934b1a0e61f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9e44cd61-adda-5320-9484-a3532ad9dce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-342321" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}+xy=6{y}^{2}.$ The normal line at the point $(2,1)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are $($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(2,1).$ Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $xy$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+xy & =6{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{y}^{2}) \\\\ 2x+(y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ 2x+y+x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-12y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ (x-12y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,1)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{-2x-y}{x-12y}\\right)|}_{(2,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(2)-1}{2-12(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-5)}{(-10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the normal at the point $(2,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-2(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =-2x+4 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ y & =-2(0)+5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b26c1af7-9a5b-5d43-b1b5-dc74de3adf12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316127} :answer-field/id #uuid "52a041cb-28c3-51b4-b579-c9bbf60ee415" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c82bb467-368a-5a4c-94f6-b887899fd25e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342355" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1aab10c5376ca6ae6c43829bc6f7617fb236b24c883d8ad61c47b83b75511d5a.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the right-opening parabola shown above. Express the answer in terms of the variable $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola has its vertex at $O,$ and passes through the point $(6,-2).$\n\nThe standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola whose vertex is at $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ and $y=-2$ into the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 4 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of our parabola is \n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}x.\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{3}x) \\\\ 2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03c73c66-958f-5ae6-bd8a-99db605e90d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315139}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315139} :answer-field/id #uuid "4184994e-4d69-5dc7-a131-80a1fe4b7e91" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316695} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008c957b-5932-51e6-82e7-283ff77f4652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315116}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315116} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc33ff76-870c-56b9-8d86-2109f98f6537" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c5c6413c-46e6-5a5d-8df1-b86b59abbe4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342377" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+x$ at the point  $(2,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88360469-59b8-5b02-aeda-8cf7e65c4609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315881} {:answer/id #uuid "ab20be1f-dcc5-5a14-9c42-22e0e5ef8153" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316083} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4dcc7b-bc08-50d9-ac0c-46d4b93351cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316267} {:answer/id #uuid "d33d7662-167a-5d11-846f-667301d801c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316303} {:answer/id #uuid "e16461ec-ab88-5ba6-91c2-989ef7110015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316267} :answer-field/id #uuid "b382dd51-d26b-5b2d-a525-b9c5fa09d000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41d7c399-26f9-5663-8418-176ada36a15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34238" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩,\\,0\\le s<\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{2}s)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2({\\sin }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(\\sqrt{2}s))} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56bbcef-ca3b-59ed-ad87-9a69dc545ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316388}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316388} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45e5e3a-6e0c-55e2-aa5c-80a5a35d6947" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53516b05-10ad-5e8a-b3c5-ada063505bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315595} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb1fb59-755b-5372-84ae-0e78b8fd575f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd3747ed-3ded-5af6-bc1a-40c4627f070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342381" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}$ at the point  $(4,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}+\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2})+6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}+6\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0)+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,\\frac{11}{2}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317268 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f6c5c73-46f4-5ddf-b01a-d3a05203e483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316013} {:answer/id #uuid "b56847e0-4b6b-5a51-985e-89beeb02f4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316146} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ef61f-6dd8-54da-bde6-17815a1082bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316230} {:answer/id #uuid "c67c58ba-476e-5959-b889-0918f9c42026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316239} {:answer/id #uuid "fa222ffa-b579-591d-933a-a85db1502118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2{\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316230} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbfc18f6-ebd0-5adb-b118-eafa57d1adc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "370f1619-74a5-5906-bb7b-088dc720563d" :question/math-academy-id "q-34245" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$ are as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,{T}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given both the derivative of the curve ${r}^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the unit tangent ${T}^{′}(t)$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the magnitudes of ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}){\\cos }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{3{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\sin }^{2}⁡t+4{\\cos }^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡t+{\\cos }^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304a723d-3566-569e-9ca2-d4132ac4e7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315390}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315390} :answer-field/id #uuid "600467cd-e097-59f7-a8a8-7ed056a91dba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92ca3690-4c8c-55b7-be9d-9370a5311a98" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c47875-1ef5-501d-bbee-a417107b5d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315433} :answer-field/id #uuid "a259aaa2-07f9-5836-91da-9db416d5277d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e562f9e3-80f6-5a8e-be6e-6fda00e93b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-342584" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317314 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8b71532-1488-535c-8b59-8f7973ce0715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316411} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f69b0ab-5955-53db-b009-8c0582376a86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4aa4c921-dada-59cb-bb50-cb4450e9d902" :question/math-academy-id "q-342689" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-{4}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21be9580-2335-54ac-8059-660a2e09e0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66056fd-b5e9-5140-97df-839aad6ea433" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9bc16a4-340b-5d92-b113-ebdd1b46f211" :question/math-academy-id "q-342707" :question/problem "Given that $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta =\\theta (t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(r) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\,(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot [-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)] \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50f5854c-de0b-5300-b587-3f4c1f05cc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315580} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d1fb93-14f8-572f-956e-21c9206bbd45" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "94b3a7f9-6cc0-5ccb-aca0-c8164b3cf667" :question/math-academy-id "q-342740" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is moving along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}$ such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is changing at the rate of $-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ Find the rate of change of the $y$-coordinate when $x=3\\,\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nThe $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x+1}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ and $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3+1}+2(3)) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+6) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{25}{4} \\\\  & =-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-coordinate is changing at the rate of $-25\\text{ }\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317322 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56c67aa2-6002-59cd-8ae7-1f2226d9333d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315620} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0393134-2dd8-5918-a0e3-a7b00514608c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "87eb3505-70a1-5321-b696-f100dbab50cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-342772" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\,,\\,y(0)=-3,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /4}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=-\\frac{3}{s+2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-3:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-(-3)=sY(s)+3\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $f(t)=5\\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing.\n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /4)s}\\cdot L{5\\cos \\,(3\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s/4}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =L{5{u}_{\\pi /4}(t)\\cos \\,(3t-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})} \\\\ (sY(s)+3)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s)+3 & =\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =-3+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{3}{s+2}+\\frac{5s{e}^{-\\pi s/4}}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317323 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "454031fb-d968-502a-ab3f-df93403f8653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315519} {:answer/id #uuid "816282ac-3dfc-57d5-9df3-d0079f794032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315831} {:answer/id #uuid "97669a6d-02b3-57ba-bba2-b6864e81902e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315961} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e3164f-859c-5ff3-924d-f64996d960a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316125} {:answer/id #uuid "b501f954-5954-54d1-a029-9d17e4f0ee33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315961} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ffaa86-ca52-5a1a-ae7b-ae3a365017ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a5173f3-affb-5348-89f6-746440382607" :question/math-academy-id "q-34278" :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s.$ Find the curvature at the point where $s=5\\pi $, if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n\n \n\n$$\nT(s)=\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,k.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the curve $r(s)$ and its unit tangent vector $T(s)$ are parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $T(s)$ with respect to $s$ and evaluate it at $s=5\\pi $: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right))\\,k \\\\  & =(0)\\,i-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3s}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\ {T}^{′}(5\\pi ) & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot (5\\pi )}{5}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi )\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (-1)\\,j-\\frac{9}{25}\\cdot (0)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we obtain the curvature:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(5\\pi ) & =‖{T}^{′}(5\\pi )‖ \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009076e7-87b7-576e-bbb7-5638940d7dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} {:answer/id #uuid "032231c3-dc3e-5b5e-8890-15f57b1864de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315134} {:answer/id #uuid "b5480eaa-19d8-5c53-8739-e8b4885f637b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{10-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316145} {:answer/id #uuid "c700e6db-2a5d-50ae-bb39-9ca51c2cb886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}-\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316243} {:answer/id #uuid "e3afda1a-1ec9-56cc-b1c0-d2d20aec3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} :answer-field/id #uuid "90fe901c-f8c8-565f-93b4-e7e2fe0bf635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d571581-f4a8-5a66-b8eb-3c5c0101beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}\\} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32db4599-5baa-5f10-ab65-2b675eadb0df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315414} {:answer/id #uuid "6d12dab3-4f94-5cb4-8998-33856cd365ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315735} {:answer/id #uuid "6ddd0ac5-d0d0-5606-a202-02d7527fe075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}+{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315742} {:answer/id #uuid "a8563721-a011-5048-9f1d-98ff988b05b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316063} {:answer/id #uuid "f6864e51-0f54-5592-b22f-b40d87e378bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316063} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bd3092d-a263-53c2-b82e-7c2cd58295bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "350fb974-2dc7-5612-b740-6ce1b236d228" :question/math-academy-id "q-342814" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-3)}\\,,\\,y(0)=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{2}{s-1}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{(s-1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s-1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s-1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(3)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-3)\\,⟹\\,B=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}({L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s-1}}-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot [{e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=2{e}^{t}+\\frac{1}{3}u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{(t-3)}-{e}^{-2(t-3)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bb86229-e35b-53e4-913f-4d749f83077c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315182} {:answer/id #uuid "78409cec-7d3a-5018-8211-374354f871e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315794} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0d4b84-a896-53a4-8a7d-99182c49f054" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315818} {:answer/id #uuid "d40d8afa-6705-55cf-8bfb-ebe80732cb6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316306} {:answer/id #uuid "ef79be0e-dda5-500a-ae0e-ddbb56647f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316450}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315818} :answer-field/id #uuid "73214da6-cfd5-55ae-9cfd-5d2071e6e2b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "01fff398-2b6d-506d-9fb7-5344ae756247" :question/math-academy-id "q-342815" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y={u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot \\cos \\,(2t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi /2}(t)=u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{s\\,{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$ \n\nHint: You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+4),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+4)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+4)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+4)+0\\,⟹\\,8A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=4A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=4\\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{4}(-\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+4}}+{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/2}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}[-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\cos \\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ and $\\sin \\,(2\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(-{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})({e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $$\n\\displaystyle y(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{4}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot g(t),\n$$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-2(t-\\pi /2)}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8de4250-244c-599d-8ad8-6dcd8ea0d782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316065}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316065} :answer-field/id #uuid "59da1fc4-190c-51ce-89c1-86dc868f0ac8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ce56e89-e4a0-55d3-bc60-994f61368da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342937" :question/problem "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}$ at the point where $x=3.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{(x-2{)}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-4x+(-2(x-2{)}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-4x-\\frac{2}{(x-2{)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =-4(3)-\\frac{2}{(3-2{)}^{3}} \\\\  & =-12-2 \\\\  & =-14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-14.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f9acd4b-e9fa-5157-9c6b-9e6e7b92d98b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-80/9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315572}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315572} :answer-field/id #uuid "904bde89-fd81-5c60-8d85-fd0811a7990e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f8271ee-facf-5afc-aacd-f55d8d375bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342942" :question/problem "Find the exact value of the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}$ at the point where $x=3.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(3).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}-7\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-6\\cdot 2x+{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}-21{x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & =8({3}^{3})-21({3}^{2})-12(3)+\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =216-189-36+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-9+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=3$ is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(3)=-\\frac{80}{9}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317344 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "565253a0-dd76-5878-a7a9-4d02c0c4fb2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315612} {:answer/id #uuid "6127abfe-b3b5-53d1-80dc-adfc13213bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315661} {:answer/id #uuid "6c430fee-5cee-52c9-aa2c-21b95ea58ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-s}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315727} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd27d68-4ff1-5483-b08a-a6cf002b0807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s-1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315741} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb0ca75-2725-5130-a280-b76152dbb5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315661} :answer-field/id #uuid "271ee920-82c3-5b6f-9a20-a71259ec1617" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316636} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d82da8d-de3b-50ea-80e3-5b6f2e301096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315289} {:answer/id #uuid "3447534b-e198-50ea-ab41-3d0bda427511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315425} {:answer/id #uuid "37e98731-46c9-5121-b094-d05fd40536d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315441} {:answer/id #uuid "daef66fb-4bf5-5c47-9422-2355ef5544b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315441} :answer-field/id #uuid "753a75d5-e5a7-57ac-9cdd-83a1b3c0d991" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23148239-08bb-571d-8ee4-d16856744176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315312} {:answer/id #uuid "2643860c-cbaf-5089-9e07-85dcc01bf2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}-\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315333} {:answer/id #uuid "59334acc-00f4-55d4-8893-99596de90306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315628} {:answer/id #uuid "adc67946-4de6-5ed2-b87e-0af0e29aaec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}-\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315312} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec9bde2a-e15f-5b58-a398-7c211684b04f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c31d5d2-d84b-5f0f-a8dc-d915106eb0d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-342961" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+2L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+2Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+1}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+1}{(s+2)({s}^{2}+1)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+1=A({s}^{2}+1)+(Bs+C)(s+2)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-2+1=A((-2)^{2}+1)+0\\,⟹\\,-1=5A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{5}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n1=A+2C\\,⟹\\,1=-\\frac{1}{5}+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}{s+2}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{5}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+1}=-\\frac{1}{5(s+2)}+\\frac{s+3}{5({s}^{2}+1)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}} & ={e}^{-2t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+2}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{3}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c15c0d-3f63-5626-8437-1d07dfb1c869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s-3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315118} {:answer/id #uuid "10b6cc8d-1ff6-5f5f-b9f2-bb50c04eb7d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315220} {:answer/id #uuid "12ce8ffd-dca9-5cac-90ca-040567267891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315230} {:answer/id #uuid "486fb27f-8afc-53da-90dd-8a970092ad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315536} {:answer/id #uuid "ed617c3f-60b8-5742-abec-ca9b550b9c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3-s}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315230} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d7fe7c-9b67-526d-84b0-aecc2edd4906" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70fc568e-cbff-5a63-b7d0-8052cbf0e641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315755} {:answer/id #uuid "8a872fca-500c-5209-a6ab-186145fb360d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315891} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c809cd-7250-5385-8318-d7158b9c34a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316507} {:answer/id #uuid "fda00ec7-5657-52db-b36a-adad840bf3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-6t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316507} :answer-field/id #uuid "587ef0bf-9fa3-50bf-b0ed-b158d189ebfc" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec4a8c2-c836-5840-b694-20dd11459345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}+\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315207} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf47adb-a4fd-59db-8eb2-1fe5160a7e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}-\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315557} {:answer/id #uuid "6f76ae1b-28b0-5e1b-98a6-6ede16ec878c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}-\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315746} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd40d37-2bf4-50a7-9ad9-c6729c9cea65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+6}-\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316200}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315207} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb0527c8-5f53-50ec-9a08-80884046b33f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ad24855-aa70-5db4-b32a-41dc702fd75a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342962" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+6y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below to find the solution using Laplace transforms. You may make use of the following results:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $Y(s)=L{y(t)}.$ Taking the Laplace transform of both sides of the equation and solving for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe partial fraction decomposition of $Y$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)={{field-3}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides of the differential equation. Using the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}+6L{y}=L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the transform of a derivative $L{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)$ and the standard trigonometric transforms\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t}=\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\n$$\n\nwe substitute the known values. Note that $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(sY(s)-0)+6Y(s) & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9} \\\\ (s+6)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe rearrange to solve for $Y(s).$ Dividing by $(s+6),$ we obtain\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nNext, we perform a partial fraction decomposition. Since the denominator has a linear factor $(s+6)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\frac{s+3}{(s+6)({s}^{2}+9)}=\\frac{A}{s+6}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns+3=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+6)\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-6,$ we have\n \n\n$$\n-6+3=A((-6)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,-3=45A\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n  \n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,0=-\\frac{1}{15}+B\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n \n\n$$\n3=9A+6C\\,⟹\\,3=-\\frac{3}{5}+6C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThus, the decomposition is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}{s+6}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{15}s+\\frac{3}{5})}{{s}^{2}+9}=-\\frac{1}{15(s+6)}+\\frac{s+9}{15({s}^{2}+9)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}} & ={e}^{-6t} \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t \\\\ {L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{s+6}}+\\frac{1}{15}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+\\frac{1}{5}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\ y(t) & =-\\frac{1}{15}{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0574318a-232d-5f69-9e15-68afe95d5fca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315145} {:answer/id #uuid "18d299e5-b7a1-50bc-b7d9-f0bfdaad9949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315263} {:answer/id #uuid "dd7cd4a5-0a7e-5edd-8d44-9fb5579397b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316350} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a1af10-480d-5cfc-b7e3-de3c57cc075d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316350} :answer-field/id #uuid "1757ae18-7f32-544c-baf6-28be3aedc3ed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316598} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11f968-2e95-52b9-a2ab-522e32c43fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315378} {:answer/id #uuid "70477c8b-d4b6-5b7f-a6f4-ca0c0ee50276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315750} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec79c86-f689-5ef9-a38e-388e8c829a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315816} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a21b35-cf30-5dec-9bec-bc167d074164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316042} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5ac8ec-cfc7-547e-8c33-48c44cf8b7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316444} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c1eb2b-9266-5913-a75f-4b83e1959f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316490} {:answer/id #uuid "f64537eb-26b9-5725-bfd2-d4080e393b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316490} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e63e251-8004-5902-bb34-58005f81b826" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aad3b08-652d-5b65-8cb3-d59a84557b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315365} {:answer/id #uuid "36fcec82-462e-57d6-b23c-fb5fa7856404" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315438} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7166c7-695b-537b-98d6-d76d2f1212cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316198}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315438} :answer-field/id #uuid "37ff6bb1-6d34-5c6b-b439-33fee60af799" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316676} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd5691f1-592b-5b69-a58c-920a167de7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316279} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0c043d-5eae-5ed2-9603-09298bc4c93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316281} :answer-field/id #uuid "44be650e-57e3-5e7f-a1b5-535d0664b43b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320a4b1e-355e-5064-94c6-57b1e59ff223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{108(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315409} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c7643d-6d9a-5e78-bd24-b6d113d550e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316250} {:answer/id #uuid "cf74292f-2bae-52c3-8212-e06bff086ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316289} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb0dae4-982c-5cc7-a4a5-d9e12288703f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316250} :answer-field/id #uuid "6098084d-9179-514b-8288-f653800985ea" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7127629f-cc39-5e9d-b607-fa5f8e083997" :question/math-academy-id "q-342963" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1$ defined on $[-3,3).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-6x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+1∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-6x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2}+1) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -6x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ -6x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c013f9b-32c6-561a-8bb7-5ffbeeb02352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{x}^{3}-3\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315804} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab75fc2-e886-5595-bce3-c49cad6edb6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bacdaf5-3165-55be-a1a2-4605e9c99c4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342970" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for $y={x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5}+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5{x}^{3}+3(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(-3)\\cdot 2{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =20{x}^{3}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317354 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9ba391-ec76-500e-a4b3-f13c4c8a9fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316270} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a8d81e-5ba1-5d3c-ab8d-a75ade40ffc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6406b0de-85c2-57d8-ad6d-cd0fcf72814c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343086" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}},$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{5}-{x}^{2}+x}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3x}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e280261-9531-503b-b9c9-1565463ee291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316003} {:answer/id #uuid "6b08480f-d7e4-4859-b212-0ecf6bf11508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2x^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673867}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316003} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806fc65-8fca-534c-b69b-882c7849b116" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "340cc804-6eed-5bf6-9664-2ced31609b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-343089" :question/problem "Given $f(x)=3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}},$ we have\n\n${f}^{‴}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x}-2{x}^{-4}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+8{x}^{-5} \\\\  & =3{e}^{x}+\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002a8adc-95e9-5fd0-9071-7bcfbfd23cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+12}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315113} {:answer/id #uuid "0a240e5c-77a4-51f8-bdd1-7487d77e28a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+8s+12}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315168} {:answer/id #uuid "35ba3a61-324c-5fbc-806a-97165ec93eaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315432} {:answer/id #uuid "573112da-88f3-5b9c-8e23-7bd12d9e01aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315624} {:answer/id #uuid "f56bc1f5-3cf7-5929-8a42-708d519baaca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+8s+15}{3s(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316489}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315432} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c357f8-4f5f-59d8-8c98-7ba8b6a9839d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7597fdf3-9405-5418-b0fa-60d2dd89b4dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-343110" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ for $s>2$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5-2{e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-2y} & =L{5-2{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},\\,s>0,\\,n\\in {N}_{0} \\\\ L{{e}^{at}} & =\\frac{1}{s-a},\\,s>a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ L{{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-(-1)}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-2L{y} & =5L{1}-2L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ (sY(s)-\\frac{1}{3})-2Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{2}{s+1} \\\\ (s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{s(s+1)+15(s+1)-6s}{3s(s+1)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{s}^{2}+10s+15}{3s(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aa045d5-9683-533e-b3b9-383180919e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c61dd80-a3fe-57a0-9d9a-a6d5e5d6f81c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b99f3255-7a91-5700-9ad5-6321b83c8397" :question/math-academy-id "q-343225" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3):$ \n\n![](images/bb/bb49c052cabe12e8869270ab61b1de9e7f45b6acd6eaa9bc0d4c6892446a7cb6.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }(10\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+3))=(10\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=5\\pi .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102c20a7-364b-5e1d-8e5b-1a8f7adb1463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315216} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a5b80-b993-5acb-b1fc-0027ff1b76db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315921} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfc4594-abfa-5c41-b497-cf307852b689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315989}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315921} :answer-field/id #uuid "5244a3bb-594f-53ba-b2ef-f72d466a2fc7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316738} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226b5654-77e7-5290-83cb-1a0492f45274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315307} {:answer/id #uuid "60c3bc85-544e-5b9f-ab33-7b566e41ce0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{84(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315659} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5f4e68-ccd2-570b-9ee9-e4c567858d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{252(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315810} {:answer/id #uuid "a9af117d-edd7-5efb-8dba-c537f16eed90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{84(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316072} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e5d328c-5ae8-55b6-b9ab-a3f56bbb2e90" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316911} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "288201e6-f2dd-5dfd-bddb-d01ad4149311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315351} {:answer/id #uuid "9c12c233-b887-55fa-88ba-f3727f0b1a7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315351} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3026fce-a564-560d-a98f-f8c84ca4478f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b4c586-dd65-581f-b72e-da973be223dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315664} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a765d5-f9bc-5b61-b6b0-ddc6438dc48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316140} {:answer/id #uuid "d8db2b4d-be1a-577e-9d71-586b8b3ff301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316140} :answer-field/id #uuid "c367b66d-d706-58a4-8744-c9027f239869" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317011} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "779b25e7-e9f1-5970-81e6-c2457d778bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315791} {:answer/id #uuid "bb973c70-a9f5-5ed1-9595-2ee7f36dd4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316179} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc1e478-54e9-577d-b817-e82797475d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316357} {:answer/id #uuid "ea83f06e-8df8-5e52-bb5b-19418d0ece66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316422} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a1345d-5208-544e-96f2-59511c4c5a07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce2d6842-c273-5f28-8b8b-7bdf00107d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-343245" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-14x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}+4∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-14x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-17)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -14x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-84(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{84(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0933967e-8a36-5c98-9978-af481d6e1c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315163} {:answer/id #uuid "6668d210-3666-5a78-bdab-b36c77b23f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315688} {:answer/id #uuid "c224d4a5-a69e-51bd-b87f-2a11fff0f518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316209} :answer-field/id #uuid "206ee1d4-18b6-5317-b71a-60ab4bb59aa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316623} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "308e0394-5c17-5c18-8ff0-e80da631f21b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315392} {:answer/id #uuid "663577a8-6abe-5cfe-8679-56b730b9aecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315687} {:answer/id #uuid "abaf8f39-f4db-51da-95b2-51924405272d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316085} {:answer/id #uuid "b09a64ee-5b6e-5079-a8f0-ce12b6d3bd23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315392} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fff564-b5cc-53b2-b1bf-779a47a33f0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67056a51-8bcc-5951-a0d0-b567453ecd31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315694} {:answer/id #uuid "f23de7db-25f2-5351-89a4-05d8a006985c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315694} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f83e8b-16b5-50e5-bd6a-69d2b2b948fc" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc8153e-6858-5649-9642-986ded05b056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315194} {:answer/id #uuid "32aafad9-b891-5160-95ba-28b9c800706e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise smooth" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315412} {:answer/id #uuid "972cfc99-791b-5cba-acea-8d970ecf96a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315412} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed857dbe-1d3a-57aa-b8bb-c3366b4316ed" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317110} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39479c33-fb81-51e0-8823-cad5f018ef97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315444} {:answer/id #uuid "5d516aab-95d3-5da5-b47b-419bd5928c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315647} {:answer/id #uuid "82484d8f-762c-5112-80df-c8f5a3a96f8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315837} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bea66d-8fb2-5f85-b391-cff23ec897a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{75(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315647} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5483c1d-8db2-580c-a05c-15ddc746e3b3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "295865d5-976f-59ac-b940-a1bbaaa4904a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343247" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5$ defined on $[-5,5).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-\\frac{3}{2}x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5∼-\\frac{5}{4}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{5}{4}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+5) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(-\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-15(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317374 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cae41e16-6610-5e58-9108-5de3295f0ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316263} {:answer/id #uuid "fa388873-f615-5353-85a4-676842681e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316516} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af30516-d825-5430-b585-c2b9688b499a" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316723} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039f2cc3-1de9-5a93-8764-48f56e6f9a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{216(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315138} {:answer/id #uuid "3995ff7a-9bd7-58ae-976a-61d050379397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315447} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6c2b16-cfd7-5