2186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316890} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c76e086-be81-5d6b-a343-be8c7050fe9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315731} {:answer/id #uuid "6f526222-4d4a-5c74-96af-91cf72e99a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315745} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f931b0-1b17-5662-8969-603cb5a2d9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316066} {:answer/id #uuid "eed71728-b46b-50e6-9eff-2edbfa25b5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316446}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315731} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c738c7-e531-5dcb-ad7b-b8948ed39455" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f546d09b-84fb-50a0-9cc7-aadc5bfa6e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343251" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=12x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-2∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n12x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼16+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=6{x}^{2}-2.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{x}^{2}-2) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(16)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ 12x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{216(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-72(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ 12x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c67e515c-772b-57db-ad7e-bc3bafd270b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316240} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40c8ae7-36d2-5187-91d9-5e9873b26864" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c8b7de5b-aac2-510e-98ee-6d02baa0c52b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343279" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+(\\frac{2}{x}+1){y}^{′}+(2-x)y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSuppose ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions and their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies $W(1)=3.$ What is $W(2)?$\n\n$W(2)=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -(\\frac{2}{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(1)=3$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(1) & =\\frac{C}{{1}^{2}}{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3 & =C{e}^{-1} \\\\ C & =3e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nW(x)=\\frac{3e}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $W(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{3e}{{2}^{2}}{e}^{-2}=\\frac{3}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317389 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30654a61-bca2-5d45-8dfd-6666c2232131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315391} {:answer/id #uuid "481d3ffb-b826-5c26-abd5-24a0975fb43c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315535} {:answer/id #uuid "57b480dc-e5e7-550b-882b-cb01488fd141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315626} {:answer/id #uuid "5a702db4-464c-52c5-ae25-6568483b6169" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315632}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315391} :answer-field/id #uuid "118aaf33-e092-57ae-ad75-e10e6a5a7996" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0369724f-0d94-5066-8c6d-06eee2316ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315136} {:answer/id #uuid "2272ec4e-6d60-5d4a-8e26-dfa44c7f7097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315308} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4d0ed2-69a5-5df4-bdc3-0fc96726b52a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315808} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c001ea-6af4-548a-b0b9-b3d01b3ebf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315308} :answer-field/id #uuid "969b70b1-fcf1-530b-977f-41d3ceacc8b2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316892} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "437a1983-ea9c-5b88-8dc2-8237185da01b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315510} {:answer/id #uuid "4604ac7b-ebad-50c4-be31-0f256f65d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315523} {:answer/id #uuid "51805e32-ba50-5870-872a-92eba551bf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315584} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ec959b-ec78-572d-a741-db60cde72fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316030} :answer-field/id #uuid "aff630a9-dea1-5c77-90b6-f67e4767911c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2afdaff0-fd03-5d5a-9b50-c71e5077877b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315370} {:answer/id #uuid "48a43274-16a8-55d9-a83a-9deaa58b42c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315537} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7619dc-5aff-5054-8800-bb71d5b9826a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315916} {:answer/id #uuid "cc610ca7-4121-59bb-8b6e-2e667e4b319e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316276} {:answer/id #uuid "f24b10d1-1646-5236-ba37-c6e42ab5dac6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316469} {:answer/id #uuid "f8779403-3b1c-590e-a881-326314ab16ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316506}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315537} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8cf181a-e3d2-5269-b318-b692a92a9f47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "01fa87ea-c90a-5da0-b692-1e4e4dbca851" :question/math-academy-id "q-343339" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-4x$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-2{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-4x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -2{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0ac3e0b-2daf-5d1e-beff-f97fddbb7155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316364} {:answer/id #uuid "f80c16b0-b617-5e6f-9cbf-e99c39b1f438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316504} {:answer/id #uuid "f9066d99-512e-552e-8140-af7ecdf45283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316508} {:answer/id #uuid "fe128583-dbef-5676-9904-6cbef28f082d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316504} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e47ea7f-e9c7-5f93-902e-56be750e1cd6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316654} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5586e6bd-914b-5563-88d9-3ff9d4e94fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315609} {:answer/id #uuid "6315c6e0-1f6e-5475-88d0-5109e5e16125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315675} {:answer/id #uuid "9a182076-bba9-533d-9110-45fd7a130e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315981} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dbfeef-607d-519a-8290-f3538a833481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316417}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315981} :answer-field/id #uuid "40007788-c6e1-5d2f-a613-1ad04f16535b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8a3c58-25e9-5f17-94ad-bc0082326c31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315201} {:answer/id #uuid "69316d08-e2d6-56d6-aab1-3d1f61b6cec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{48(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315710} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d7ef19-fb4f-5d33-83d6-08a0e589010c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316057} {:answer/id #uuid "cf32c093-f419-533a-8c1c-53b8651f43b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316286}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315201} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f081ed1-3b16-5b9e-bda2-41b1fdccacc4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b381952-9b35-5658-9a1c-bb7343908f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315548} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5e8d78-0c39-54db-922c-1b42e53eb152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "piecewise continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315814} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0cb57c-2c6c-506c-a631-df11bc6136e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316181} {:answer/id #uuid "c87d645a-8c67-5b22-89fa-a4a0b393bafa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315814} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc26870-1dfe-514a-a70b-a4e5da432c5a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3b75f000-c288-5650-b319-990a54e2ce6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343340" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}\\right)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b796236-ecb8-5476-9123-68c2471bcce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315549} :answer-field/id #uuid "66aeef22-5905-5dc6-b02a-1f2b21fdc21a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "883d77a7-ed55-5648-ad28-2c6a5e33fb4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343359" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\pi .$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\pi .$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=3 \\\\ {f}^{+}(\\pi ) & =\\lim_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(\\pi )+{f}^{+}(\\pi )}{2}=\\frac{3+(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0037eb03-213e-5d10-a8b1-2c3b156f0fd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315114}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315114} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab6fb759-4189-56fa-94b4-4e7de6084501" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97ff3547-7e44-5c45-a91b-58643d43344d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343364" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2}, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 1, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & =\\lim_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff08d19-8cb4-5226-967f-942523bd104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1a59b6-d6c2-5e99-8bc5-ed1d49da21ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "086a93d6-9edf-5e6c-82a6-6ae6a865d444" :question/math-academy-id "q-343370" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a90ff20b-46bc-563c-8a6d-2698d67747fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316068} :answer-field/id #uuid "49330cc6-9572-5a0d-b1f2-3e73f8b2ef51" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d4ef002-5026-5196-8b56-541878d83ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343372" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ {x}^{2}+2, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=-2(-3)+1=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8d60e5-0ad9-55d5-8b7e-e475ef95acb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316010} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b06dbb1-77e5-5ad6-bd29-3766a587c036" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85b0a574-008a-56b0-af42-48f4058807a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343385" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=2\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a066781a-6e52-5220-93ef-23ff199bd9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316022} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f46bb7-8b99-5788-8b85-2ae0679bb181" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc552628-2cac-58c1-ac79-840e54e9a1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-343390" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4, & x\\in [-5,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,5)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $10$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(-3)=(-3)^{2}-4=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9288273b-d288-5f04-89d9-be38e43d86fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9eb9028-95ee-5fce-9d8e-0984b5b70969" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe7adb62-e3df-5206-9031-d8f651922dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343404" :question/problem "The depth $D,$ in meters, of a plant’s root system after $t$ months is modeled by $D(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2t+13}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months?\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $t=6$ months is\n{{field-1}} meters per month." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is equal to ${D}^{′}(6).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{2t+13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (2t+13)^{(1/2)-1}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2t+13)^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{4}(2(6)+13)^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the root depth when $t=6$ months is $\\frac{1}{20}$ meters per month."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ef83eb-7903-5ed1-889b-def0c697eddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315133} {:answer/id #uuid "27e8af18-0de0-586a-9eba-abc08ba0860f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315346} {:answer/id #uuid "39b7289a-e50b-58ae-adf6-ed3e51b77d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315449} {:answer/id #uuid "da376413-1d3f-5fc1-a97f-75c417a4de92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315133} :answer-field/id #uuid "7464d8a5-08db-527e-ae6f-a39637e25763" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ed0276-2c2c-590f-8cf3-8e9560a9382e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315332} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0beb9b-15d0-5e7d-be81-a2261768c844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315464} {:answer/id #uuid "83f4701d-d6ed-5779-9953-ace7e06d74cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315849}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315849} :answer-field/id #uuid "86734426-13ad-555c-959c-201c8b396e42" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d7be75-9cff-5055-abf6-8731caa29961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315715} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb1ce19-9f8b-5eba-83bd-79edd557b6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315715} :answer-field/id #uuid "c07a2823-4eda-5d88-8515-cf12f86486ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103a311b-6837-5aa1-b2e0-7d1ceba394e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315217} {:answer/id #uuid "711120c9-6920-5a47-9c36-a481b0f8923f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315759} {:answer/id #uuid "c3328fd8-a582-53de-9cd2-efd19d1100c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316215} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a5aafe-207d-58af-a17d-6e2bb04ce27c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316363} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9feec9-f3c6-5510-bc50-0395fa9771a9" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0d801752-c780-5add-95cf-b3a0d0a59bd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343429" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\sin \\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,\\left(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot 1}{2}\\right) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3303fe09-6ad0-505a-89d8-a19574971d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315415} {:answer/id #uuid "69514901-5c64-5d07-b95a-d45b7fa88118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315712} {:answer/id #uuid "a1035538-7e14-55b0-ac2d-8d7e5266d7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316026} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b0e268d-0a7c-5c87-942a-0d527b2e7230" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316610} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "526509c1-9276-5c3d-8d48-c9645209f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315588} {:answer/id #uuid "55ea1062-c9e1-5062-8d07-1555f9a98009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315611} {:answer/id #uuid "b78b7f35-d5d7-5ae4-be39-18600507be7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316159} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0c938c-160a-52cd-8dd0-6140a987639e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315588} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f1fe62-5fe6-5d2f-a137-b4f3a4585631" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316633} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b65ed1d-642e-56f2-bfbe-4ef0a45f2f92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315459} {:answer/id #uuid "b855acb5-e97c-5841-8d72-85aa1b7718a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316161}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315459} :answer-field/id #uuid "32132a2d-5a62-506e-a3d9-84e46c8a7381" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c35aee6-7694-50b8-99ff-38af1b21180b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315189} {:answer/id #uuid "13531dbc-f785-5e13-9d29-9d280d8db742" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315233} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9a9a9c-8530-5ab3-8a17-1e2278aba947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315567} {:answer/id #uuid "9666b55d-0e86-50aa-b086-53a192b7da15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315567} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f23a1a7-b2fc-50dd-b7d3-dea39ae4eda7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0b3adfef-3238-5b0a-8f95-7aed0a3d3235" :question/math-academy-id "q-343443" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -5, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\sin \\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin \\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))=\\sin \\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot \\sin \\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\ 3 & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4de519-7dc3-5a63-91dc-88fb097de927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315559} :answer-field/id #uuid "80e5b3fd-f8d6-55a8-9a84-9bc4da788fc1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9c255544-1d5d-5734-8a75-e3f42b3984e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343679" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.8\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=2\\cos \\,(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.8{y}^{″}=-ky+2\\cos \\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{4}y=\\frac{5}{2}\\cos \\,(\\frac{5}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{4}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\frac{5}{2}\\cos \\,(\\frac{5}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{5}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{4}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}=5\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca9f77e-1eac-5dd3-b2b8-a6a15bf83357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} {:answer/id #uuid "282a52cc-8658-5a56-b555-abd92b1826a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441790}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316277} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fa88ca7-4486-52c4-a8ca-dfdd856acf42" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7756f2f0-f8ff-5a38-a573-589b8cc562e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343691" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.4\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.4{y}^{″}=-ky+(4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-2).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{5k}{2}y=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{5k}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=5(2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)-1)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{2}{5}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{18}{5}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136b6bc8-8153-5df8-a7bb-b1c0b8f33d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315235} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eaebf75-d5e2-5d17-abf3-5de30e8eb341" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5563b807-3ce9-515c-a365-9e78582b198e" :question/math-academy-id "q-343738" :question/problem "Consider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=4,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 3-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012ce73b-ccad-5736-8a98-79181d781399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315120} {:answer/id #uuid "2b42cc41-9300-5600-adab-14f258488843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{1/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315371} {:answer/id #uuid "7903666a-0bc5-545c-8cca-acb01e2c736b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315796} {:answer/id #uuid "c16bf73b-f3b4-5ef9-bf8c-52d15609bd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316204} {:answer/id #uuid "c85c337a-02ca-5277-ab4f-284e2592cc3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316204} :answer-field/id #uuid "517a7074-69ee-57e9-839f-2627066da920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8320061-9385-5e97-8a78-62a775ef29e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-34381" :question/problem "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317443 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c374525-0d10-5050-a300-a79dcfbe1344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315379} {:answer/id #uuid "30db2edc-6f08-58d8-870f-a953379c323f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315396} {:answer/id #uuid "68b802ec-a75e-510e-92f8-96b459e2052d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315709} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcf3171-8f8f-58d3-b834-e5adb23c8e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316539} :answer-field/id #uuid "18004c40-7610-5c60-8808-ea9e4b8d8d4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f91b7c-20bf-51fd-a393-6190c6c8e355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315338} {:answer/id #uuid "3890626f-6d86-591f-9835-41dc0785b07c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315443} {:answer/id #uuid "c23d6f0d-4068-5f82-916d-ff3b5f243150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315443} :answer-field/id #uuid "58145300-29fc-5a90-a84c-4bf31179dc08" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316750} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0962404-85c9-5e1a-8305-8c121059d764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316458} {:answer/id #uuid "f48b2e93-6f81-57a4-9d61-664c45eb47e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} {:answer/id #uuid "f95e153a-270c-5fbb-9ed6-cc3b5cab48dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{24}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316510}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316483} :answer-field/id #uuid "c413fdfb-1b7f-5850-8061-58d3e5250a37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae77a2f6-2db6-5433-96eb-14517b11c7c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343854" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n} &=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}\\right)\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e72c91-6260-57d4-8f2b-d360cd74d1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315345} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0f4d4a-2783-5d38-996e-52ca4cc7173c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316256} {:answer/id #uuid "e004ee5f-c254-5252-8869-1073aea1cb7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316361} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8de36c-f73d-5178-bafa-3d6b6a68abaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316543}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316256} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50cf4cc-94a7-5eeb-8680-cbc2d70101de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d266502-5760-5926-ac9e-cf2e92540f0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343875" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}+9y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the free term and all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)+9({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms to find a common denominator:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(9-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\left(\\frac{36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{b}_{n}(36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}{4}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi (36-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646ce3b-5090-551d-9fb1-bf4ec6deb32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315150} {:answer/id #uuid "4da7a4cd-9b3c-5f25-984b-86a574f36a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315560} {:answer/id #uuid "842c68ff-d986-5b83-8ed4-916bda537f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315851} {:answer/id #uuid "91d7a856-b77f-50d1-88be-950876c5c0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315560} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa5c5b2-3fbe-541c-8cfb-3a77ebfc14d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316575} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32be831a-09d9-5501-b835-67a87d1101b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315413} {:answer/id #uuid "8554200b-b663-52a0-8e3d-3817e84aed0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315860} {:answer/id #uuid "99d579a0-0202-5771-83ae-1712b9cbb47f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315977} {:answer/id #uuid "c3858021-49df-5890-b7d5-30813f99a2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315860} :answer-field/id #uuid "3230a8b4-805e-5348-a21b-273d105379f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f5c097d-b8ef-5e78-80f1-563c7c1e4885" :question/math-academy-id "q-343879" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)-4(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right))=4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-4\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right)=4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=4 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{4}-4) &=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n} &=\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+16)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbe36d7-183b-52f7-83e4-766f66e47bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315183} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4ed8e-1bfe-547e-be15-ae3378443b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315562} {:answer/id #uuid "7d958b5e-134b-50cf-8131-f9c56b793d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} {:answer/id #uuid "98d3973b-e604-55c9-a982-051d4e5c7549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b16506d-2a67-50c2-9faa-8d223beb5eb5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316761} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a66bfc5f-3122-5a00-a1eb-aba1035e251b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} {:answer/id #uuid "ca234db0-bfef-5bb4-bb9c-30e03379c192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316257} {:answer/id #uuid "dccf7745-d3a5-5591-b2fd-26d0e4c875cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316347} {:answer/id #uuid "f080bbb4-00b0-52bb-883d-abd8921bc331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316457}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316055} :answer-field/id #uuid "c113cb3c-937d-5360-937b-d1f8edc5d5c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cffe765b-9372-5c49-a0ae-c98bc95f8843" :question/math-academy-id "q-343880" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right))=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right)=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=3 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-3) &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n} &=\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{27(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+27)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "474ff160-2d86-5c69-8f85-60a047da5ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315534} {:answer/id #uuid "708fd803-efd9-508d-a532-3adcfc75816c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315751} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c52c8f-3852-5f92-80be-6b94f3557303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316242} {:answer/id #uuid "ec8a1480-b239-5433-afdd-61831369aa0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}