es:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)-8(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)=8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=8 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-8) &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2} &=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n} &=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+8)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0775efd4-349e-5347-a0ab-209c8617865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315155} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6860ca-41e8-5f7c-9922-b06de92ebf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} {:answer/id #uuid "a683ea58-3eb4-513d-ae1d-5c5ee8f548e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "f37afc9b-a1f3-5052-8c94-74b4bd652d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} :answer-field/id #uuid "69639b9f-3bb7-58ec-a83d-c19f4137e2fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8e5254-d482-5016-83a5-2d062da9f632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315193} {:answer/id #uuid "464643b7-419c-5207-ac7e-9b750b301b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315524} {:answer/id #uuid "85cff5d8-8660-5f09-837d-b9a7c81dfdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f486c4-6a97-5a85-bb1d-c46b9ac5c8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2fdc4d-025d-5256-ba3f-588326c84d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a67d7600-1f79-59aa-9051-5cda440b9948" :question/math-academy-id "q-343901" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A &=0 \\\\ \\pi B &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=0 \\\\ B &=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5229753e-9f7f-5a27-b3ee-c4a1ce8dde6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315586} {:answer/id #uuid "862b0cf3-99de-5116-b9ab-82a11436ad9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dff446-91ec-5570-8171-c24135981f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316299} {:answer/id #uuid "d9600449-c9b9-5aec-ba8b-15aa0966e5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316328}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315868} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87d8604-1b8f-56e7-a069-08f0654c46b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d996731-b251-5b34-875b-e57bb6c2b7c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315290} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5d9269-3d3c-576a-b059-ab2e729a7735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cc4c52-e7e1-5f1b-ab95-62c5cbd519d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316484} {:answer/id #uuid "fce744b4-f140-59b2-84b8-c7432217b040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{15x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315453} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb46e197-216d-53cb-9e25-46577a9846a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "de35c174-f52d-54b1-9320-5816b5069f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343905" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation $$\n\\displaystyle {y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right):\n$$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30ec1724-e787-53ac-b7be-da19606b22cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315398} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8c06e6-1c97-596f-a48b-c8e3a08ea23e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5c39cbb-58a7-550a-8949-adbe8e453b51" :question/math-academy-id "q-344033" :question/problem "Consider the section of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the ellipse at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x}{16}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y}{25}\\right) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2x}{16}\\right)+3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2(3)}{25}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of the ellipse.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{256}{25} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{16}{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}\\left(\\frac{16}{5}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2d6b70a-1c88-5078-851d-20d14bc97f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316130} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1a5d6f-aeae-536f-a6b0-a2ebf0eb504b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6bac51-594e-5c2f-9f7b-596126ee5877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315646}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315646} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0afef72-f33d-5803-bb02-33e8a33d9345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16454d27-1535-5826-aeea-dc3e22b21a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-344059" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+x$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{16}\\right) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=-16\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-16\\cdot (-1)>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-16\\cdot (1)<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ we conclude that, in ascending order, \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n are indeed points of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40cfd269-a13a-5c49-a56d-cc8ceb8431d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315492} :answer-field/id #uuid "77bf0f74-1242-55a2-a21a-8980e84f9457" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "182e0255-d720-51ea-a804-063597c68a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0e86915-59bc-51c8-833b-92056390e32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316025}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316025} :answer-field/id #uuid "68bead88-75be-5d98-8a3b-321b58f5fe4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316799} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "988f23a7-7155-5992-b06c-b475653fcc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315968} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf6207b0-fbeb-58fc-9587-10adc79effbe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a4c8fd4a-9b9a-57a2-9a9d-092a924402b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344091" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ has roots at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](images/26/26374f7e5c86c5585d384b3e65b4a1b3fb2f94172ed753cdb3fe7bdb0af0abb8.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ is positive when $x<\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x>3.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b37edff-c855-58c8-a1e7-fc99abe16c16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315547} :answer-field/id #uuid "47b68395-3502-52e0-95af-6d1e2cfc51fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ab20c9c-58a5-5fdf-8875-282abcda9969" :question/math-academy-id "q-344110" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b564f91cb71a17a1449e1ff723e32f450da6304d9c3ba33cd01e2ff942bc25c.png)\n\nAs $x$ approaches $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ from the right, the values of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are positive and grow without bound. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\infty .\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a149596-45cb-5497-baab-8a480b36b3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315980} :answer-field/id #uuid "185a3b06-7b04-557c-9acd-c47afbe20d98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43806c2b-bb8d-5959-9b41-a6ace76db4b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344144" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{0}{1}-\\sqrt{3})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fac581ea-573a-58c1-b5bf-d867db6529a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1275}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316522} :answer-field/id #uuid "3820224b-6d56-57e5-aac2-f726f0b03ec6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eec707f8-4b36-53bb-89b3-e76de724d729" :question/math-academy-id "q-344298" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{32}\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{32}{x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}{|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & ={\\frac{5}{8}\\,{x}^{8/5}|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & ={\\frac{5}{8}\\,(\\sqrt[5]{x})^{8}|}_{1}^{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,((\\sqrt[5]{32})^{8}-(\\sqrt[5]{1})^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,({2}^{8}-{1}^{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}(256-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1275}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c63c4de-0dab-52bf-8c24-fa965cfa4bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315992} :answer-field/id #uuid "f778a30a-c5c0-5352-b673-8509c4dbf394" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bb2dd7d-277e-5f99-834d-beebdf7df217" :question/math-academy-id "q-344401" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317507 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6ee50ad-f5e6-5ccc-8f77-df111921b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(2∗{2}^{1/3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316318} :answer-field/id #uuid "151c7883-0bac-5441-a408-c45dbb1501af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "36e19ea4-7147-564a-aefc-cb94da408789" :question/math-academy-id "q-344503" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{16}{x}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2/3+1}}{(-\\frac{2}{3}+1)}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3\\,{x}^{1/3}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}{|}_{1}^{16} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt[3]{16}-\\sqrt[3]{1}) \\\\  & =3(2\\sqrt[3]{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f4af314-ad30-59f0-b11b-c562e39b68f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315298} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e53595b-957d-54a7-8438-697a6c71e89d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fff3a1da-bf05-5bb7-8c2e-7cfcf86c187b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344521" :question/problem "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfies the mean value theorem for integrals for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+3$ on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n$c=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the mean value theorem for the integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-a)f(c) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{4-1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(2{x}^{2}+3)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+3x)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}+3\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}+3\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{164}{3}-\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(51) \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =17 \\\\ 2{c}^{2}+3 & =17 \\\\ 2{c}^{2} & =14 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =7 \\\\ c & =\\pm \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $c\\in [1,4],$ we take only $c=\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56fed0a-d8f9-5c20-b0a0-6a076d63dfbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316389}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316389} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e11ff29-510f-539f-968f-2aea79bad506" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3ee597c9-1d21-553e-8a89-39a826142bb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-344578" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}\\tan \\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](images/ca/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}\\tan \\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\tan \\,(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed2abe4d-57c0-599d-b2da-3bc3a8e57a74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316437} {:answer/id #uuid "9019925a-fde9-5e42-9741-5b09732117ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516414} :answer-field/id #uuid "492b509c-6314-53ae-a2da-64e59ff1f2cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce1dc838-ac3c-58a3-9be3-5a30e3211516" :question/math-academy-id "q-344695" :question/problem "Find the average value ${f}_{\\text{avg}}$ of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ on the interval $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}\\right)|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }[(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-1))-(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13e3bddb-3f9c-576e-a683-adad59e375a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315237} :answer-field/id #uuid "49f84908-ceb0-5d12-b800-1a1ccc31bffa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "74174cfe-6c69-5c75-8eeb-a70591a0502f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344699" :question/problem "A biologist found that the population $P$ of some algae in a container can be modeled by the function $P(t)=150+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),$ where $t$ is the number of hours after $9$ a.m. Find the average number of algae during the period from $9$ a.m. to $3$ p.m. Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{avg}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the function is valid starting from $9$ A.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,6].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}P(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6-0}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(150+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}(150t-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\,t}{3}\\right))|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(150\\cdot 6-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 6}{3}\\right))-(150\\cdot 0-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{3}\\right))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(900-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))-(-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(900-\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\cdot 1)+\\frac{30}{\\pi }\\cdot 1] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(900) \\\\  & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad936be-e38b-53f3-95da-1981252a3aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315368} :answer-field/id #uuid "49eb6ffd-24ea-565d-ae7a-66af08b3af52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0ce4b2b-546b-5d49-899e-91e594c8d459" :question/math-academy-id "q-344744" :question/problem "![](images/98/9854b8b086ef6f60f02a37f2a0885cbcdd41ec24d5548abe1aa2d6fbebe8dbfe.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is being inflated, causing its enclosed volume to increase at a rate of $128\\pi \\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius increasing at the moment when the radius is $4\\text{ }\\text{cm}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe radius of the balloon is increasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the volume of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=128\\pi $ and $r=4,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\ 128\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 64\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the radius of the balloon is $4\\,\\text{cm},$ the radius is increasing at a rate of $2$ $\\text{ }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b5a17cd-3acf-534d-972e-8478d4c1c034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315721}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315721} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2268b2-2b75-5cce-afa3-a8423cf68bb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2dafc830-96ac-5240-a52a-f840915b50b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-344749" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1aafeb4abd5ee1ee545474ed353bccbfb0986cefccb33779fa5744d735d1bdd.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated at the rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If at a particular moment the balloon's radius is changing at the rate $\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{ cm/s},$ then what is the radius of the balloon at this moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $r$ when $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{ cm/s}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi },\n$$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi (r)^{2} \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius at that moment is $4\\text{ }\\text{ cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317554 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e567ffed-bd0a-5d3a-b232-703e5d9daa33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316387} :answer-field/id #uuid "6863f71d-5e56-5993-accd-b64eaaf7c653" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1bc9d4-eeb3-54c4-8202-c0899576f21f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315998} :answer-field/id #uuid "e02e7ec7-31c5-5afe-9dea-0953cd757b7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04a4e002-f8db-5f15-9950-79b6717520bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-344842" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}+12x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-15x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-5x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=1$ and  $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc2c744c-7a76-5ef7-800d-02fa38b5d996" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316275} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa1ab18-27d3-535c-97cc-6e784e524a62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "262ea3e2-0286-569e-9bb1-c84346f66411" :question/math-academy-id "q-345037" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\sqrt{4x+8}$ between $x=-2$ and $x=2$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $8$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $i.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{R}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is   \n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=8$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=-2$ and $b=2.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-2)}{8}=\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(-2+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}f(-2+\\frac{i}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\,\\sqrt{4\\,(-2+\\frac{i}{2})+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}\\,\\sqrt{2i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:answer/id #uuid "42412c56-59d1-5d81-93a2-a4f22e569838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{4}\\right))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{4}\\right))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317781} {:answer/id #uuid "c89f6d96-4f48-59f9-9118-1bcd54a69aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,i+(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318223} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e74fb8-0b30-5bb5-a2eb-7bf2759fc0e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R(s)=(1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,i+(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{s}{2}\\right))\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ae2ebe4-b1fa-53cb-91ec-3954d234058f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{8j}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317875}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317875} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6343721-d153-5ed8-b2a6-b958d83de090" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c79e5161-d947-5c86-9bc7-0f8d96dfae7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345048" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-3)$ between $x=1$ and $x=3$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $n$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ in sigma notation?\n\n*Hint: Write down the expression in terms of $j$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\n$$\n{{field-1}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=1$ and $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum ${S}_{R}$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{R}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f({x}_{j})Δx & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}f(a+jΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[f(1+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(1+\\frac{2j}{n})-3)] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\frac{2}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1+\\frac{8j}{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "466d07aa-1ec9-5db3-a792-07caf8fef7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{e}^{-4t}-5{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317795} {:answer/id #uuid "69a682d9-7c0a-5085-84fc-2b283b2b81c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5e^{-t}+8e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498970}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498970} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a8a57c8-14d4-5721-92e8-993aec1910ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a15ce12f-67af-595a-8feb-031dd1000329" :question/math-academy-id "q-345088" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-4t}+5{e}^{-t}+\\frac{9}{17}) \\\\  & =-5{e}^{-t}+8{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a094-335a-586d-8c3d-03c43fed7e31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318224} :answer-field/id #uuid "26fca3f8-096e-5758-9d4d-a36cc9cf460a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e95a4d25-6c9d-57b1-8d08-3197c0c476a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345094" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)={e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{-2t}-3{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{4}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-2{e}^{-2t}+15{e}^{-5t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{36+64}{25}}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79fa1d8b-57fa-5cc0-8d8b-c38952b89a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317978} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c980fd-df74-51c9-9b6d-5d9f5a80923c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e99f439d-ed1a-5653-bd3b-a57694b6cd74" :question/math-academy-id "q-345095" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(-2{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{5}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{12}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=2{e}^{-t}-3{e}^{-3t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{10}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{24}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{10}{13}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{24}{13}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{676}{169}}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90513a9a-0af3-5328-8ab7-114c3064041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{65}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318056}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318056} :answer-field/id #uuid "473aef29-5766-5445-bef5-bc608ba98aae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d2c69af-dd17-5c0b-85fa-4c79b0a97b43" :question/math-academy-id "q-345096" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is\n{{field-1}}\namperes." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{12}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=-8{e}^{-2t}+6{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{60}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-\\frac{25}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{60}{17}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{25}{17}\\right)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{4225}{289}}=\\frac{65}{17}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318975 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57be75d5-8240-5b01-9208-f359500dbe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317859} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9fc0de-285c-5603-8b7d-70406531ad81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12e^{-3t}+12e^{-6t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498918} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e443742-3007-547f-91b1-6dc76f4fb385" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9538c7cd-06c1-5575-86de-72f831f368ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-345120" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad1f1e6-67f7-5ceb-83fb-9d36d123d761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317811} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed89474-bb5f-531d-8b05-a7fbc3018b85" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f5d86cb2-121f-5b07-970b-849f480d7ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-345207" :question/problem "A body of mass $8\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(t)+\\frac{8t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39bb6fd0-ab83-5955-9da3-4caab22d47c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317751} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb0d01-55ea-5aaa-a85d-5bfb232facd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4258571-e2ee-5711-b11c-465f2f2d9827" :question/math-academy-id "q-345321" :question/problem "![](images/21/2102626f331aaa7803462f8e427ef2c0150deb554955199f3d402123934e815a.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-(\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{2}{3}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{12}-\\left(-\\frac{25}{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22becc43-412f-5475-8240-cc9fab0899e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317672} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f0e0dbd-d208-55a4-a113-35d6427cdcbf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6b35b94-1570-5f29-9f5d-68c5388281d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-345322" :question/problem "![](images/66/66bf8c1144de1ad65938345d2937fddd6fb43ec4822a2b98a9b5383ca2609567.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-2{x}^{2}+3$ and the $x$-axis between $x=0$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2{x}^{2}+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+3x){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{2}{3}+3)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee21fdb-4f31-5662-8173-652df2970035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317766} :answer-field/id #uuid "43d3cb79-dfa8-57a8-8ad1-d8a7c894979b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a5942b5-b345-55e9-98fa-6a0c078bad31" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c1b4cd5-ccb2-520e-a710-ec328eb28719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318038} :answer-field/id #uuid "b00f9c6d-32be-5c17-86d5-8ee817e9d8d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9acdaefd-0b19-529b-95bd-f2d420ca911c" :question/math-academy-id "q-345343" :question/problem "![](images/34/34eb73b6bd93f9a45b666ed0599719a09b54948d1d81b14ceea9b46f99b75054.png)\n\nFind the area between the curve $y=-{x}^{2}-1$, the $x$-axis, and the line $x=-1$ and $x=1$ (shown above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{-1}^{1}-x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3})-(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{8}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "418f9337-5384-52da-981d-31d9661f516d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317777} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf3ec70-b28b-5016-ba4e-0a5a05b82943" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af180c34-7e0c-5b7b-9e5b-5a6251599f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-345381" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dce89dbea62566c0ee897bffe0638458970799bafdc7d7c8e5084328fef38bef.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=-1$ and $x=1$, as shown above. Find the area $A$ of the region. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\arcsin \\,\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\arcsin \\,\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\arcsin \\,\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is \n\n$$\nA=-{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2d87a7-d2fa-5498-bf33-ce150d8c90ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317823} :answer-field/id #uuid "34d1abfd-cd09-5972-9ceb-09e2aeb99b48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f7a6b52-3478-5ae0-b5cf-e85d41d024b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-345382" :question/problem "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t$ meters per second.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}}  $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-8t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 2{t}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35c42a8d-9700-5030-b556-07be6636569c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317736} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb13732-f7e1-5a3d-b046-62e986b68617" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cf0fa979-937b-587d-a91b-d1227e9d68bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-345391" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $$\n\\displaystyle s(t)=\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12\n$$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{4}+\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+6t+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-6t+6) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+t-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-2)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=2\\,\\text{s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aace116-779e-5cd7-87fe-2a773103e41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1fe8d6b-b674-5235-b645-ef3a0cb2483c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af8d7c5c-930c-59d3-b49e-ff7666f63383" :question/math-academy-id "q-346081" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{\\pi x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319068 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "769b09c3-8e5a-5e55-99cd-e126e57155aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317972} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff5d4bc-02ea-5674-92d8-9925921dbed3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a00832dd-966d-5a5a-8da4-9f3ee1f87bfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346094" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}-(-2) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8bcc6d-5e6e-5f23-9931-9ba109153e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} :answer-field/id #uuid "0de184e0-4c2b-55bb-bae9-b8061c599b03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318456} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f64ae7e0-64b1-5f21-9e91-7ee063a78db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318378} :answer-field/id #uuid "92bf9fa3-b3c5-557c-b2c0-e0fba9edb465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18197a01-d08b-5472-bcd0-342b90f62fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-346179" :question/problem "Express $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[((\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{3}+2($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1,$ the lower bound is $a=0,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f\\left(\\frac{2i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[({(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)}^{3}+2(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}\\,)+1)\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523bf0fc-ebd7-58a3-9b52-197502fe9ddd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317838}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317838} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1ccd09-6638-5c02-b930-dfbbcf4d87a3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5abd54b7-37f0-5de9-b538-a6c3fecd5733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317874}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317874} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccfeeae4-8f75-5920-ad58-13968804e466" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "aad0a1ec-7573-56fe-9f5c-ec39ca09c20a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346180" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={2}^{\\,{x}_{k}}+1.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}({2}^{x}+1)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[({2}^{\\,2+3k/n}+1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={2}^{x}+1\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36401cbc-a3be-5f14-933a-695fb7aa1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317738} {:answer/id #uuid "67167783-6967-593b-8769-38aed2426b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504211}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504211} :answer-field/id #uuid "426b9925-da9c-542a-ad67-7fc57c5f23ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9ab51aa-b753-59b0-bcee-a4fcc0134e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde9f548-1427-5fca-b7bf-a0e28dfaf74b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "91c3be0f-8f8e-52a1-9627-d3cf3af82a0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346190" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319081 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb43744-0323-56da-b37a-bc808d7f0c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+8)^{1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318100}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318100} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d82930b-56a1-5c05-a0b6-4293322787f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318550} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6a0318-c314-553d-8972-0c050e18b7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317933}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317933} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c576f3-9bcb-5562-aebb-a9f68a0ffa05" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c0de2e03-fab0-563b-b613-a64d3498b437" :question/math-academy-id "q-346201" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-(-1)}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-1+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=({x}_{k}+8)^{1/3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{((-1+\\frac{3k}{n})+8)}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+8)^{1/3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fff2859a-d2cb-52a9-8ffe-178ca2dac981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318420}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318420} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda0157c-f631-5a59-a636-d361af09d12c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e255aa7-628b-536a-a6f2-d07dad3f6cc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317994} :answer-field/id #uuid "fea52e24-bca1-5064-88d5-349aec057b32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a241686-3534-59c6-96f4-590d3f608c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-346204" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{3}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{3k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{2+\\frac{3k}{n}}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b3a545a-f6fc-56d8-b618-306dd6c9b24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317656} :answer-field/id #uuid "2169b17d-9b76-5e27-8f32-b49b8393f666" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76bb0795-0840-56ba-91a7-84e2ebc07a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-346217" :question/problem "What is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+1},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3i}{n}\\right)+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[\\,\\sqrt{\\frac{3i}{n}+1}\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97eba4bf-c61a-5ead-b70c-b0c6a574c1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318082} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7607952-3788-5270-8d12-8f194fc55ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "38e97327-3fa6-5865-86a2-b52a48dcf2d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346226" :question/problem "Let $f$ and $g$ be continuous functions such that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-4,\\,\\,{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=6,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=-20.\n$$ What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's simplify one of the given integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{2}{3}\\,g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6 \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the adjacent intervals rule and substituting known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the sum rule for definite integrals,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{-3}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-4)-4(9) \\\\  & =-46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =-46-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4g(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-46-(-20) \\\\  & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "946295eb-8a35-5555-bd13-62f1ed3cee60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318070}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318070} :answer-field/id #uuid "53956442-7fbd-56ac-afac-f69b2f40d66a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2af97986-d4fc-5710-a371-6bfcbb64bfc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346249" :question/problem "A rectangle has an area of $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](images/cd/cd9fa71ea72045881c25c80de4f6d8b294b60d92ea4e4f27170d68849ce31431.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $450\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=450.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{450}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{900}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{900}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =450 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{1800}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})=\\frac{1800}{(15\\sqrt{2})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{2})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{450}{15\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{30}{\\sqrt{2}}=15\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{2}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{2}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{2}\\,cm.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{2}=60\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d32187-3404-5141-800c-86550cdc685e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317842}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317842} :answer-field/id #uuid "65df60e9-ee4d-57d7-906d-dddf132074a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7477e8bb-dec7-5056-9ba5-eac2a2896c95" :question/math-academy-id "q-346253" :question/problem "A rectangle has an area of $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ What is the minimum possible perimeter ${P}_{\\text{min}}$ of the rectangle? Do not round your answer.\n\n${P}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: Let us call $x$ and $y$ the dimensions of the rectangle. \n\n![](images/cf/cf3d29b794698ff529da4c21f6fd02b3487d1650ec84b3c1f992280616f7701d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: We need to find the *minimum* value of the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2(x+y).\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: The constraint is that the area is exactly $675\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.$ This gives the equation\n\n$$\nxy=675.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Solving the above constraint equation for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into our expression for $P$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =2(x+\\frac{675}{x}) \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1350}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We solve the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2-\\frac{1350}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =1350 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =675 \\\\ x & =15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 6**: We confirm that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=\\frac{2700}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{2700}{(15\\sqrt{3})^{3}}>0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${P}^{″}(15\\sqrt{3})$ is positive, we conclude that $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ is indeed a local minimum of the function $P(x).$\n\nFinally, when $x=15\\sqrt{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{675}{15\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{45}{\\sqrt{3}}=15\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the smallest perimeter are $x=15\\sqrt{3}$ and $y=15\\sqrt{3}.$ In other words, the rectangle with the smallest perimeter is a square with sides of length $15\\sqrt{3}\\,ft.$\n\nFinally, the minimum possible perimeter is\n\n$$\n{P}_{\\text{min}}=4\\cdot 15\\sqrt{3}=60\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5eb82788-efbe-50de-b226-6bde5d6aedf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317886} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ffe2b0-f398-575b-afe8-d12d64c3f4fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec7ce12f-cbf4-5c03-8053-383589db4d45" :question/math-academy-id "q-346368" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we let $u=\\frac{x}{4},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $2\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /6$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(u))\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =4(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)) \\\\  & =4(-1+2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319095 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4fb092-4b4b-5222-a34c-724a53131d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{112}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317820} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3e3bf-4990-5b89-bf6a-9355ad43fa17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7fd825e3-0d14-5ed8-a4b9-9f4979a0fb9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346390" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=2x+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{2}(u-1).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1/2$ | $3/2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{3/2}4x\\sqrt{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(u-1)\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}({u}^{3/2}-{u}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{(5/2)}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {4}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3/2})-(\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {0}^{5/2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{5}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14e0f54-e96d-5872-93bf-ea86a07ecf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318206} :answer-field/id #uuid "526aef95-e8d0-5c8b-bd07-14ed0e710e6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "11e654cd-30e9-5558-8788-bee1abdebb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-346393" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x+3.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=u-3.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $1$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{6}\\frac{3x+1}{\\sqrt{x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3(u-3)+1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}\\frac{3u-8}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{9}(3{u}^{1/2}-8{u}^{-1/2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{(3/2)}{u}^{3/2}-\\frac{8}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & ={(2{u}^{3/2}-16{u}^{1/2})|}_{4}^{9} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {9}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {9}^{1/2})-(2\\cdot {4}^{3/2}-16\\cdot {4}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 27-16\\cdot 3)-(2\\cdot 8-16\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(54-48)-(16-32) \\\\  & =6-(-16) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "512f4400-0135-5c62-80b6-50169108ff90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317833} :answer-field/id #uuid "305c9143-4e47-512f-bcf7-7904e3678db7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54b385aa-9795-5ad7-b92b-a662f02e6b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346401" :question/problem "![](images/94/94ef52e454545335132d467773c0b274296a7cb3758ae9fae3242876c8063f5f.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ bounded between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}-3y$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=2$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{3}{4}{y}^{2}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,-\\,3{\\int }_{2}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{2}^{4}\\,-\\,3\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(\\frac{64}{3}-\\frac{8}{3})\\,-\\,3(8-2) \\\\  & =14-18 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $|-4|=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e406c8b4-fe2c-53cd-abdc-dc32a0608267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318327}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318327} :answer-field/id #uuid "c443a9b7-8c24-5662-be21-7a311e519118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ba9633b-5871-51f9-8aee-a3691915151d" :question/math-academy-id "q-346451" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578ea0f9-6e27-559f-8d20-f7994570e05b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317858} :answer-field/id #uuid "35bbb5d1-0990-5ae6-9af4-dfc7a684e8ee" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284de721-bcb0-5584-98b1-7d0cec8bc940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317690} :answer-field/id #uuid "9565b1c0-a7b6-54b9-9567-456ae91899dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "198c9f13-854c-5a78-a110-5897652614aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-346519" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{artanh} \\,(${{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C,\n$$\n\nthe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{20}{25-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{3/5}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3/5} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})-4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡0 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f286994-9b4f-5dee-b8a9-1575e6938302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318106} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a98dba4-54f8-5f32-8ce5-f4039b42cddc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4b4d889c-3125-509c-8bd0-37836a63b86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 1\\cdot \\,dx=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}u{v}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}v{u}^{′}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,(x){|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}x\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3}}{4})\\,\\left(\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4})\\,\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)]-{\\int }_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-x{|}_{1/4}^{{e}^{3}/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(2{e}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d6ede0d-96bf-5c5d-94d9-3bfc9530489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317990} :answer-field/id #uuid "32524a9d-c9ba-56fe-bb7b-33746bfd1e70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f2a42ce-7bdd-5df7-a90b-4527797f0f5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346530" :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF(2)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nNow, computing the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}({t}^{4}-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-(\\frac{{0}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{5}-\\frac{4}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{5}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf938a7-6848-5556-b412-127df79e4fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\pi -\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318356} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e0eb84-c3d8-5f8e-99c8-0cb5dcedd949" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c916ee0c-43c4-5e1c-ab73-9f1719924580" :question/math-academy-id "q-346546" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+14{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+14{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14({\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x)-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14\\cdot 1-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(14-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(14x-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[14\\pi -\\frac{5}{3}{\\pi }^{3}]-[14\\cdot 0-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}] \\\\  & =14\\pi -\\frac{5{\\pi }^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af226b09-d827-5c1c-aaf8-a979730d39f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318157} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15b0103-0a30-53b1-b24e-a7de7389c80b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9846049f-0423-59a8-8569-7eab74f54e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-346555" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}4{\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n$F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{\\sec }^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd785ee6-6715-53f7-8cdf-ba030fe99857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318306} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fb330fb-3336-561a-98fa-cec7e2718d25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2bb34ddd-c2ee-57af-a0a2-9fb98a895f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-346575" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{\\cot }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the identity $1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡\\theta ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =x-\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\cot }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ {\\cot }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{\\cot }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(1-{\\csc }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}(2-{\\csc }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cbf723f-187e-5a64-9f3b-c073a56499af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317983}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317983} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f3175d7-30f9-527f-aa96-d5b89eadab1e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "48e677b7-771f-574c-af49-3e95d535ddf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346580" :question/problem "In $2010$, a group of biologists started tracking the population of wolves in a mountain reserve. The initial population was $250,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $6+2t$ and $2+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2010.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of wolves in the reserve at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)=\\underbrace{6+2t}_{\\text{population increase}}-\\underbrace{(2+0.5t)}_{\\text{population decrease}}=4+1.5t.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of wolves $T$ years after $2010.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(4+1.5t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =250+(4t+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =250+4T+\\frac{3}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=250+\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}+4t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89b417d9-2e3f-5dfd-a3c2-f8cc34958f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318030} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f827cf5-02b9-51d3-a2cc-ba98cf68235c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b623d6eb-0fce-5aac-ba26-711c0546ffa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-346582" :question/problem "An engineer started tracking the amount of electric charge stored in a battery. The initial charge was $75$ coulombs, and the charging and discharging rates are given by $6+{t}^{3/2}$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of hours since the beginning of the observation.  \n\nFind an expression for the charge $C(t)$ stored in the battery at any time $t>0.$\n\n$C(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $c(t)$ be the rate of change of the charge. According to the given information,\n\n$$\nc(t)=\\underbrace{6+{t}^{3/2}}_{\\text{charge increase}}-\\underbrace{(1+0.5t)}_{\\text{charge decrease}}=5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$\n\nLet $C(T)$ represent the charge stored $T$ hours after $2015.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nC(T)=C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(T) & =C(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}c(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(5+{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =75+(5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =75+5T+\\frac{2}{5}{T}^{5/2}-\\frac{1}{4}{T}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nC(t)=75-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}+5t+\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bde44ba-f514-5459-bd78-472491b1d9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318099} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d1bb4f-dd87-5830-a5c2-797b88b5c778" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "03370258-b60b-5182-80c5-13bf5b04ada7" :question/math-academy-id "q-346606" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{e}^{x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{-2}-{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={e}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{e}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/4a/4af4a2b7389d3773cc71e843190c6ab111355248a15e6165a8e39e37252061b9.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}4{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7f1b72-9b42-5b2e-926a-1a2ae744fa24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317882}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317882} :answer-field/id #uuid "6efdb32f-b871-5658-bfd1-30d010cfe658" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "609b4352-6bf8-54f6-ba10-486e4bcc6a92" :question/math-academy-id "q-346615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[{5}^{x}]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(5-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{x}$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }{5}^{x}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }{5}^{x}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/83/83f7d663fa66184b56c4f79d61f6e17012714a3de7512202b3022e91d4325437.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}{5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2683dbd-770b-5aa1-abec-75a65b0e9c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318119} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbe05ad-5699-583d-9f30-0fdb7ad65e92" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "695fa5d3-07df-5ef4-9c06-adb52aee0852" :question/math-academy-id "q-346618" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{5}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{5}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }[{5}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={5}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}=0$\n\n- $\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{5}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/c0/c0c7699b036ebd8779cd6c3d3143cdcca6312834ee731e144e38488124164f43.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{5}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d6ea70-7030-5f64-ab21-54447f69edd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318275} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c37205-17fd-5bc2-9017-7d7a7709a5a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b3b0b076-b835-59ae-a7b6-3a733887db90" :question/math-academy-id "q-346621" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-5$ and $y=11.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](images/d2/d2398c3a8a04d005dd4d0c6e8b06046a6ca98aa86127204e7521341213dfb464.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5 & =11 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/ca/caf1c4d5df49aa18ed85158615be19745abb7c078269dde35f4e57d57fae292a.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=11.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(11)-({x}^{2}-5)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319130 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cc18ef-29fd-5fe2-9809-b97825094b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317868}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317868} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d09426-4734-562c-bec6-4f2513d10440" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3b41f79-b110-546e-abb0-181ed99a24a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346643" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/c5/c5cd702bed84fd0ffc7f0e3a9510bdb3411bb8f77424786f9445c4d559392416.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-4$ to $y=4.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{2}-6)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{3}\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}-6y\\,{|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(64-(-64))-6(4-(-4)) \\\\  & =16-48 \\\\  & =-32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is $|-32|=32.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f84526-5984-5f4b-a97c-e2b0804eb9dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea54a217-d971-5870-bbde-57491a127b19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2eb01ae-aca0-5076-9d1d-17516ca61cf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-346674" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ of the closed region between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\le 0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{4}-16) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y({y}^{2}-4)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}y(y-2)(y+2)({y}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/03/03737836a352eafaf486e7e5f71d882ac39715e788a0c9113eb809b185584bdc.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $x=\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-2$ to $y=0.$\nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{5}-3y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{16}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{-2}^{0}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}{y}^{6}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(0-(-64))-\\frac{3}{2}(0-4) \\\\  & =-2+6 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cc8ab50-9678-5fe2-a058-74e9262eb4f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317660} :answer-field/id #uuid "b596f4a3-fa7b-57e3-b7e2-1fe01583da6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6f3ba61f-934e-53ed-a21e-8c527c008d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-346702" :question/problem "![](images/78/7808295803d04d2c996ca7d36110f070a60f4491107ad1c831e6fa8ea9f85678.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\pi y) & =1 \\\\ \\pi y & =…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},… \\\\ y & =…,-\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{5}{4},…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)$ and the $y$-axis from $y=-\\frac{3}{4}$ to $y=\\frac{1}{4}.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-3/4}^{1/4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4}-{\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)}{\\pi }|}_{-3/4}^{1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)]-\\frac{1}{\\pi }[\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da371f43-276c-5b18-9da5-af0ba94cea69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318292} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d176162-d1f8-59b4-b435-50afc62c79e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f963953-4ca0-5b72-8632-5bdad8051d11" :question/math-academy-id "q-346710" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-4}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+2=A(x-4)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2 & =A(4-4)+B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =A(0-4)+6 \\\\ 2 & =-4A+6 \\\\ 4A & =4 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{x+2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x-4}+\\frac{6}{(x-4)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+6{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{6}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-4|)-(\\frac{6}{2-4}-\\frac{6}{1-4}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-3-(-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e87bd15-a35d-58d0-af05-9bb9a671fe10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318050}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318050} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c8d832b-f870-50a5-9e1f-6f1980b0df63" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2de816ec-a60f-55ae-ac40-4e164bd6326f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346735" :question/problem "Find the area $A$ between the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}}$, the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{x+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\ {y}^{3} & =x+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ x & ={y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}\\,|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot y\\,{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-4)-\\frac{1}{4}[-1-(-2)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-4|=4.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d279eda-667c-555e-8386-f505329564d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}3-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317986} :answer-field/id #uuid "30bd3f50-8128-5d2f-a7f6-94157ad1e225" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68d214ab-9577-5e8b-9200-4e23d1e48429" :question/math-academy-id "q-346752" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-1)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx-4=A(x-1)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4 & =A(1-1)+B \\\\ B & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4 & =A(0-1)+B \\\\ -4 & =-A-3 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{x-4}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{3}{(x-1)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x-3{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|\\,{|}_{2}^{4}+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)+3(\\frac{1}{3}-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{1}\\right)+3\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9086928b-d274-516e-9dba-84121d192d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318058} :answer-field/id #uuid "63a1068a-8792-5bd6-8fa6-7396c2438b9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0202626-0cd9-5ad9-969e-84650f47d2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346783" :question/problem "![](images/81/816b36f644f3e41d8f48bed2dd49e150d751cb13e082b6a1b9b4ad16973d3d57.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ between the curve $y=3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}}$, the $y$-axis and the lines $y=1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{x+3}{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} & =x+3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-3)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-3{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,|}_{1}^{2}-3\\cdot y\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{3})-3(2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}-3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtained a negative number because the region lies to the left of the $y$-axis. So, the area that we want is\n\n$$\nA=|-\\frac{20}{9}|=\\frac{20}{9}\\,.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25ee2626-1199-5d03-8d29-49c8714aadba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317684} :answer-field/id #uuid "41efcb76-531e-5d4e-bbdc-2d53d1b9757d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a319b5d2-0ac1-592d-b731-73b0e1569f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318124} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab4aa44-1646-5ef0-a44c-3eb1d7c25c9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9e232935-891f-5034-b933-fe05b7b0f382" :question/math-academy-id "q-346793" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $+$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $5-2x$ |  | $x+3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{A}{5-2x}+\\frac{B}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(5-2x)(x+3)$ gives\n\n$$\n4x+23=A(x+3)+B(5-2x).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)+23 & =A(-3+3)+B(5-2(-3)) \\\\ 11 & =11B \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)+23 & =A(\\frac{5}{2}+3)+B(5-2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)) \\\\ 33 & =A\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right) \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+23}{(5-2x)(x+3)}=\\frac{6}{5-2x}+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3292633b-30ac-5c02-bb00-561e642ae309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317723} :answer-field/id #uuid "2861d4ee-99bc-55fd-980b-a15c95a09bea" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318506} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97177ef5-a170-5955-bfe0-424ef6f22683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318081} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d65071-bc35-56ea-b328-5b09965b48e3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fb85f9-4ad6-5774-bff4-0de9fba7fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318129} :answer-field/id #uuid "c233b400-2ce4-5120-a868-2448b5aac6f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2b918d3-6ba6-56f0-80d2-8d7b4daa7976" :question/math-academy-id "q-346796" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x-1$ |  | $x+2$ |  | $2x-3$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{A}{x-1}+\\frac{B}{x+2}+\\frac{C}{2x-3}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+10x-21=A(x+2)(2x-3)+B(x-1)(2x-3)+C(x-1)(x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+10(1)-21 & =A(1+2)(2(1)-3)+B(1-1)(2(1)-3)+C(1-1)(1+2) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)^{2}+10(-2)-21 & =A(-2+2)(2(-2)-3)+B(-2-1)(2(-2)-3)+C(-2-1)(-2+2) \\\\ -21 & =21B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{2}+10\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-21 & =A(\\frac{3}{2}+2)(2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-3)+B(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(2\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-3)+C(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+10x-21}{(x-1)(x+2)(2x-3)}=\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}+\\frac{3}{2x-3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319143 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cdd9318-65a7-533c-9343-8e00fc9c94dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317612}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317612} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecbca350-fe91-5e26-911b-b885d997937c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0bd17bf2-3389-5d0e-862d-dbaaa48c1784" :question/math-academy-id "q-346846" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [2,5].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{3/2},$ $a=2,$ and $b=5.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{({x}^{2}+2)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+2)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}={x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{3}+(5)]-[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}+(2)] \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319149 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4433636-de54-5170-a857-396d891596aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318131} :answer-field/id #uuid "31383c4f-cfa9-5007-b57d-2e5cd4edf56b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c20fd1-e5d0-5ba9-99bf-7df4f0871ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317848} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a98a53e-0ccc-5bb0-9b9b-c7afded015e5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ac6e641-ebff-5ba3-96ab-3a7269a51765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317654} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b92fd65-9ef1-54b2-ab0f-14aec51ef8f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "807c4b87-85e6-5eed-9e1d-1e100a9b4aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-346957" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8({x}^{2}+4)$ |  | $8(x+2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =C(4+4) \\\\ 5 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((0)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =2B+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2B & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+\\frac{5}{4})(1+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =3A+\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\ 3A & =-\\frac{15}{8} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{8}x+\\frac{5}{4})}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{8}\\right)}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\,x+10}{8({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{5}{8(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e62c0ae-d8d1-54c4-b59e-37ff1225c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{40}{7}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317998} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b15ad3-db26-57a4-ae32-bd15d4341f5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fc7feda-9975-5f67-aaa7-81f6387a8c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-347077" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)} & =\\frac{A}{(x-2)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2x+2=A(x+4)+B(x-2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)+2 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-2) \\\\ -6 & =-6B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+2 & =A(2+4)+B(2-2) \\\\ 6 & =6A \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}=\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-2}+\\frac{1}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{6}\\frac{2x+2}{(x-2)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x-2)(x+4)|{|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\  & =\\ln \\,\\left(\\frac{40}{7}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319153 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f33917-c3bd-5b12-9b6b-0ce6f8b325a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}(t)+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576} :answer-field/id #uuid "a200f1e4-6bd5-5695-b6f2-1b9d16078323" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02080071-87ef-54d7-ace2-86ed19a7a892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}(3t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5791a6-a1f2-5cc2-8148-a1c683f40b10" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da0cb5eb-230c-5c67-a533-8ac850a1bfba" :question/math-academy-id "q-347095" :question/problem "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\n$$\n\n and $F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨4,\\,-2⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨4,\\,-2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1⟩.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60fb6abd-fc18-5787-a50a-f7753e54cc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317894}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317894} :answer-field/id #uuid "dda12e46-a850-53be-837a-09ad3f3a2528" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18752a3b-4e21-5a37-b7a8-b41615c40257" :question/math-academy-id "q-347099" :question/problem "| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $7$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $5$ | $6$ | $2$ |\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,15]=[0,4]\\cup [4,7]\\cup [7,15],\n$$\n\napproximate the area $A$ under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/a4/a42b281338ce0fc7d64677b260f6c0d8cdaa47855afe35bae0c21fde750a39d5.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(0)+f(4)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{1}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{1\\text{st trapezoid}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(4)+f(7)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{2}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}+\\overbrace{[\\,\\frac{\\overbrace{f(7)+f(15)}^{\\text{bases}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,\\underbrace{Δ{x}_{3}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}^{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+5}{2}\\cdot (4-0)+\\frac{5+6}{2}\\cdot (7-4)+\\frac{6+2}{2}\\cdot (15-7) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4+\\frac{11}{2}\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+\\frac{33}{2}+32 \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b79c6239-a2b4-5adb-bff4-04a647c99f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1ff960c-dde6-51c4-9221-8694e66bf0d3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1effe8ba-c512-5938-b5b0-96467f99280a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347129" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a}\\frac{1}{(3x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)]}_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{a} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/9f/9f882cb5102270dfe4bef256782c18aaeeca3518f564cd188298040237e30f62.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/9}^{\\infty }\\frac{15}{9{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3a)-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab9b813-e82d-5a38-ba61-89626d7d1c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317935} :answer-field/id #uuid "815a6349-427b-5e3a-a643-711d994ac65e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ed35df0-dd3e-544b-a8f9-e8b1427ad2ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-347131" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1/2}^{a}\\frac{1}{(2x)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =18\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]}_{1/2}^{a} \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/2a/2ad762128a32c119f1783b88b4d10ae6820a914689296f03eedad206e097a29d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{\\infty }\\frac{18}{4{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{9\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bbbcd2e-e49b-5990-8260-a2931be2caf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317938} :answer-field/id #uuid "040618a6-eddd-51a8-850f-68269a30ef6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d52f629d-5761-5095-9e24-5fb415966e4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347151" :question/problem "Use the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=\\sqrt{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,⟹\\,2\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=\\sqrt{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{3}}^{a} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/28/28afd0a065525cd225377165c2164a1ecc254b1be8aba60d64324d14430c3be4.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡a-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d522c0d-ad10-5b06-9fe3-2989a9dd3f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318102} {:answer/id #uuid "140301e9-e5d7-5ea2-aa4c-0f869d4dbf85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436650} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d38f5c3-8667-50d7-9b7a-1d44eaf041bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "06eac076-500f-5f71-9fa2-51a0ac0ba8f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-347169" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}({\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall  ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ so letting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x) & =1-{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)-4{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =1-5{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =3x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x))={\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(1-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)-\\frac{3}{2}x)\\,|}_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}]-[\\frac{5}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}] \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]-[0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}] \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af67922d-f985-5063-b19c-20d087c931b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318158} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4f061a-ff83-54a7-966d-5615c8fefe75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436651} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ce878db-88ef-5b01-8a34-4af432d272f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6626a560-3774-509b-81a9-784bc902ebe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-347179" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b163cd4cc1add41ae2c043b4b3d7d818dd9b23de0a4b762bc85d348c0bca4def.png)\n\nFind the area $A$ of the region in the $xy$-plane that is bounded by the curve $y=3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\pi $ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Do not round the answer\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+{\\sin }^{2}⁡x & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+({\\sin }^{2}⁡x+{\\cos }^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1.\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2x)\\,|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot (-\\pi ))+2(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(0+\\pi )-(0-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\pi +2\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319162 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1199ebe-a0b1-5a40-a2a5-427db8b4baa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi (3-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318257}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318257} :answer-field/id #uuid "04fab8e1-494f-5e77-b310-e874bdc8871c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b3e8c15d-8f18-55a7-9ed8-7cb93c2d4442" :question/math-academy-id "q-347182" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta $ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2{\\cos }^{2}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )+\\pi -\\frac{1}{3}{\\pi }^{3})-(\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -{\\pi }^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (3-{\\pi }^{2})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd2fbc9f-48a1-5c2b-89a1-307d2139891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318245}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318245} :answer-field/id #uuid "17522d8d-7810-5302-9bf6-5882f90fc718" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318476} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25025d9d-6420-5cf6-8c09-373f27ddd609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317681} :answer-field/id #uuid "77181306-41f2-5174-930f-602a8930d3da" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8163d066-3daa-513c-b43d-293ab7fb7657" :question/math-academy-id "q-347187" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨7{t}^{2},\\,-18⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=5.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$v(5)=⟨${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-18)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14t,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=5,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(5) & =⟨14(5),\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨70,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7d255f-76ed-5d4d-9c15-25da71cd5728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317871} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff050c97-a6d3-556d-b091-7af92711e18e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b7d4a2c-14e4-5bf2-b20c-66932dfe09d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347189" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1),\\,\\frac{3}{t}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed $|v|$ of the particle at time $t=3.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe speed at time $t=3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=3,$ we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =⟨\\frac{1}{3+1},\\,-\\frac{3}{{3}^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed at $t=3$ we calculate the magnitude of $v(3),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v(3)| & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{144}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2907d204-aa9c-5112-8656-1e30df760d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317693} :answer-field/id #uuid "b82aae19-64d8-5e4e-a9aa-0b036284f149" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "59ebaac7-5149-59c8-b6c1-4ccea72bf0ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-347191" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves in the $x$-$y$ plane (relative to a fixed origin $O$) with position vector\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡((t-1)^{2}+2),(t-1)^{3}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((t-1)^{2}+2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-1)^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2},\\,3(t-1)^{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1, \\\\ 3(t-1)^{2}=0\\, & ⇒\\,t=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=1$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df512bf2-90fc-547c-a250-4964bbc1b39a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{t}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318312} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e594be-ed9c-506e-ba07-f08b9c76f216" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318570} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cc2d77-f358-511b-85d7-961c4871ff5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317869} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dbee47-9757-53dd-9775-18a9e7fe254f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88cd8173-f11f-54e6-8cfc-fb63702e9e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-347193" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-2t,\\,{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2,\\,4{t}^{3}-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a11000-64b6-5231-906c-696851af98a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6t-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317683} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e050d9-7478-5128-83df-f28e3a8dcd96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d836af55-22c0-5b01-8881-fcddd7c13345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318284}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318284} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f2d172-a249-5e7a-81f7-2711f2e87d1d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f975283a-d853-55b0-bb90-1390051f81be" :question/math-academy-id "q-347194" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}(t-2),\\,t(3-t)⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}(t-2)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t(3-t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t,\\,3-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-4,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d020359f-ff21-5101-919a-89fcfe8947f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318255}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318255} :answer-field/id #uuid "2165b78e-e685-5887-b09a-7911dffa4b52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318494} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d958081-55ce-5c60-9ae9-1bc9fc1b47f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318043} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9f5a57-f211-5303-b15b-31433ee0df5c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "10977b26-093c-5635-8ab8-f131bddda146" :question/math-academy-id "q-347197" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+1,\\,4+{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=0.$ Do not round the answers.\n\n$a(0)=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4+{t}^{2}-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-4t,\\,2t-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t-4,\\,2-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨6(0)-4,\\,2-6(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797bc2a6-2341-55dd-873d-4b6ac0c84ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317977} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f8ca8af-f6ee-533e-b29c-ab3346bd7b7f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318518} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ab4558-3a85-5515-bef0-c07596fb4c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318169} :answer-field/id #uuid "974cc391-37e1-57a4-94d9-469fa48eb9be" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318759} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3046fc10-9d94-5a65-9f58-734286a4697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317711} :answer-field/id #uuid "c351f8a2-9df9-5565-bda3-25f3df12e50e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a788bb34-e61a-56de-b053-7c383ccbe7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-347485" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x) & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cos }^{2}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{\\cos }^{3}⁡(x)} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ and ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+3{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{5}\\right)|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a003a1d1-8ee3-58b9-861c-8527f9522a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318112} :answer-field/id #uuid "2837f48b-1a7c-56c4-a073-cfae4a30010a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fcea307-0381-5580-bd2c-78e4cb1e7349" :question/math-academy-id "q-347621" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=5+{x}^{8}$,  then $\\text{d}u=8{x}^{7}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5+{x}^{8}}\\right)|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5+0}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\lim_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{5+{a}^{8}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{7}}{{(5+{x}^{8})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231add23-4a78-5c94-9dd0-b509f14aa041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317675} :answer-field/id #uuid "323c40cd-ec74-5e03-9d9d-d11e4aaaf111" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71e2d2fe-db56-5215-bda5-50cd85f9ff3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347669" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $22$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $64$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{3{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{1/3}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{a}^{64}{u}^{-1/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {\\frac{3}{2}\\,{u}^{2/3}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{2}}|}_{a}^{64} \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{64}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 16-0 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2/3}^{22}\\frac{1}{(3x-2)^{1/3}}\\,\\text{d}x=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57db562f-825a-5c06-9c18-a4185051c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317861} :answer-field/id #uuid "5672edce-69fb-5725-aa5f-85f880ba3571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "95cfa877-83ba-5877-86e4-7c000e0d1f2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347839" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\hspace{-0.167em}({\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{6}})={\\color{blue}1}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}(3x) \\\\ {\\color{blue}1} & =Asin\\hspace{-0.167em}(3\\cdot {\\color{red}\\frac{\\pi }{6}}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9304c318-6557-5b1e-8c3f-d8ee29bd0f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318066} :answer-field/id #uuid "539e41be-6ade-5d2f-b1e9-282fb6a4299d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "872456bc-2a6f-5558-8687-33dde5dc89a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-347893" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y,\\,y(2)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(2)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4(2)-2(3) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ | $2$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d92b06da-fb3c-57c5-9235-dbf59949e7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318288} :answer-field/id #uuid "9631cd73-4b95-54ed-98f9-00abde9aab73" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d73910c-7f55-582f-8a8c-b82dd58bd693" :question/math-academy-id "q-347894" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-1,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Fill in the empty box in the column for $Δy$ in the table below using this method. Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\  & =(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95c32748-1b84-5ed5-959c-dd6d7145b2f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318076} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd1677a-d348-59fb-af69-0081ceeb77f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3x-\\frac{x^2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530204}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530204} :answer-field/id #uuid "3be6d7a8-1952-57cb-b022-4c09477de6f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d5e6eca5-858d-583d-9a91-3b9faf2fe102" :question/math-academy-id "q-347899" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "The Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $3x$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & =(3x)-\\frac{(3x)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(3x)^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{27{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{243{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯ \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{81{x}^{5}}{40}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{24}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the expansions for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =(3x-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+\\frac{81{x}^{5}}{40}-⋯)+(1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{24}+⋯) \\\\  & =1+3x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df05bca7-208b-51f0-99f0-8edecf87d5f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x-4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318310} :answer-field/id #uuid "992460e1-3f67-57eb-93a5-8ffcb85ad3ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "114576aa-ad09-56e0-b6a1-dc47bf7aa7fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-347901" :question/problem "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4x+4{x}^{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4x+4{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4x+4{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1+2x)^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin series for $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x) & =(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3}-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{3}(8{x}^{3})-⋯ \\\\  & =2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the previous expansion by $2$ to obtain the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+4x+4{x}^{2}) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2x) \\\\  & =2(2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯) \\\\  & =4x-4{x}^{2}+\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{3}-⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b40aa56b-ab70-5e10-858c-cf521e2db7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318173} :answer-field/id #uuid "b308216d-b811-5250-bc3b-cb96f7740d70" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0a9ebacf-0ee8-5c1e-ba6d-9dd55f05b2b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-347941" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{9}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /9}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}{|}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e5c10b-7c01-5c7b-9d33-25a5d00db333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317573}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317573} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc45187f-7242-5bdc-b654-92b1e7393aff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2762faf0-2591-5ef5-a12e-a574a607e5fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-347970" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-8n}{n}+\\frac{7}{n})}{\\frac{4n}{n}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8+\\frac{7}{n}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319215 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df1244f-2478-59fa-9205-3abb6b316471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317663} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb23aac3-080e-5517-9268-7c05bf4177f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c3444195-6ec4-547a-bc65-bb90b0249884" :question/math-academy-id "q-347979" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $3{n}^{3},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{n}^{2}+7}{3{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{5}{n}+\\frac{7}{{n}^{3}}}{3-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{3-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6abdb46-d245-5e10-aab7-c59e7ba8d8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318138} :answer-field/id #uuid "b21be80f-3841-5060-b293-f9f1bc8d0e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "954e2492-e474-5294-a874-53e54830b1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-347981" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{n}}{4+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b110b0d6-c194-5ef0-a3c7-642b089c9adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318165} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfa024cf-6ce2-57d2-a10e-96cc60b66e60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6af93ac4-cbfc-5fc3-987e-15888aeb5e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-348002" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(5{n}^{2}-4)(5{n}^{2}+4)}{{n}^{2}(3-40{n}^{2})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(5{n}^{2}-4)(5{n}^{2}+4)}{{n}^{2}(3-40{n}^{2})}=\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-40{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{25{n}^{4}-16}{3{n}^{2}-40{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{25{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{16}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3{n}^{2}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{40{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(25-\\frac{16}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{3}{{n}^{2}}-40)} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-0}{0-40} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-\\frac{5}{8}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f4e9444-8a3a-59d2-94ed-612febf96853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317952}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317952} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17cac4a-9afe-5765-a140-52661f72aff8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ae5d8a06-6e86-5ea3-b261-bf7cda55c15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348004" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(3{n}^{3}-5)(3{n}^{3}+5)}{{n}^{3}(2-27{n}^{3})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(3{n}^{3}-5)(3{n}^{3}+5)}{{n}^{3}(2-27{n}^{3})}=\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-27{n}^{6},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{6}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{n}^{6}-25}{2{n}^{3}-27{n}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{9{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{25}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{3}}{{n}^{6}}-\\frac{27{n}^{6}}{{n}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(9-\\frac{25}{{n}^{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}}-27)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-0}{0-27} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319220 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34c22e-7be0-5daa-8a5a-7dab1587871b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317604} :answer-field/id #uuid "06bc0e7b-4af5-5c35-b37b-0d2d6d762b7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "93f4c545-059b-5e20-8b84-a274fb2b472d" :question/math-academy-id "q-348009" :question/problem "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}-3)(2{n}^{2}+3)}{{n}^{2}(5-12{n}^{3})}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply out the numerator and denominator of the sequence and get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2{n}^{2}-3)(2{n}^{2}+3)}{{n}^{2}(5-12{n}^{3})}=\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nSo to find the limit, we will need to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-12{n}^{5},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{4}-9}{5{n}^{2}-12{n}^{5}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{5}}-\\frac{12{n}^{5}}{{n}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4}{n}-\\frac{9}{{n}^{5}})}{(\\frac{5}{{n}^{3}}-12)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0-12} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges and its limit is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96314937-c536-5bae-9e39-cf844b51de53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318078} :answer-field/id #uuid "52e1c87e-231d-557c-9bd5-7ae92841d688" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0877a5c5-e4d1-511a-acaa-d1f39d42fd10" :question/math-academy-id "q-348093" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{{x}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe84162e-d333-5851-a508-a490c0ca645f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318416} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd63f7e8-8e0e-5bd5-ba90-69de5619b802" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d933376-15c0-578c-8849-2ee3600d3ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-348107" :question/problem "![](images/70/70c737e1fbbf63b3551763e48f4d7a956ffcc04ff68cced4f813b28e87630eb3.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbbd214-a055-53f2-b3b5-67bd9aed6a47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee152229-0df5-5943-83fa-bcee1ecff76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9a109daf-d742-5b15-87da-27961e643b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "639260c0-53c6-59b3-b1e6-e1986d9bc6a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317904} :answer-field/id #uuid "5974d30a-ec36-5215-95ba-60fa8dcde0ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85532a8a-4f40-5913-9cd2-e0f803e535aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318019} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a671c7d-1105-5123-bb57-8d5976a419d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318680} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e39ced00-b89f-5c02-9cc9-48217c75aed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318321} :answer-field/id #uuid "921ca22d-21df-5fb0-b1b9-8be6064c63ba" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ad040194-971d-54b7-8f33-bceb77563250" :question/math-academy-id "q-348121" :question/problem "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of  ${\\sin }^{2}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)=\\frac{3}{4}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,-$\n{{field-2}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}\\,-$\n\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\frac{1}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\frac{1}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)={\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=2\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-4\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ ${f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ and ${f}^{‴}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{(-1)}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}+\\frac{(-2\\sqrt{3})}{6}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea0640d9-c3e5-5ddf-9df9-feeb259286d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b191dcb-cda9-53a5-951a-047fd95e08bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03e05686-97cf-5c15-b403-a4caf1953954" :question/math-academy-id "q-348275" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=6x+6.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that $x=\\frac{1}{6}(u-6).$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-1$ | $1/3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1/3}\\frac{7}{4}x\\sqrt[3]{6x+6}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}(u-6)\\cdot {u}^{1/3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{\\int }_{0}^{8}({u}^{4/3}-6{u}^{1/3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{1}{(7/3)}{u}^{7/3}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{(4/3)}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}{(\\frac{3}{7}{u}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{4/3})|}_{0}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}[(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {8}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {8}^{4/3})-(\\frac{3}{7}\\cdot {0}^{7/3}-\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot {0}^{4/3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}(\\frac{384}{7}-72) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{144}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{120}{7}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319242 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "730ade57-923a-5b77-b9d1-e09ab5c9256d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317960} :answer-field/id #uuid "acfb67b2-49ba-5e80-bd44-691f651b8f48" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4037d146-183a-5606-a4b5-d0636799ff9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348282" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{2}+4x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+4\\,⟹\\,2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $-3$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $-3$ | $-4$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{48(x+2)}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{({x}^{2}+4x)}^{3}}\\cdot 2(x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{-3}^{-4}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =24\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2})|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & ={\\left(-\\frac{12}{{u}^{2}}\\right)|}_{-3}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{(-4)^{2}}-\\left(-\\frac{12}{(-3)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{16}-\\left(-\\frac{12}{9}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319243 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f92673c-93e1-5be6-93ae-6ffaadf6d7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318055} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48ddeb2-9295-55b0-9f0d-6c6f6b11577f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eae00a34-daa5-5a67-90d9-6bc0c0ecdb5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348283" :question/problem "A particle $Q$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t\\ge 0$ is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ At time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ the particle is located at the position $x=1.$ \nFind the position of the particle at time $t=\\pi .$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is equal to its position at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\pi ].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(\\pi )=x\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\pi ) & =1+{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =1+{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =1+([-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4})]-[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})]) \\\\  & =1+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =1+(-\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)+1) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1 \\\\  & =2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=\\pi $ is $x=2+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99be5e4f-a330-5237-9aa1-2288b56102b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}(2)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318091} :answer-field/id #uuid "32768bdd-f28f-51ab-bf18-de06146d3a9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3f15199a-7d19-5480-bc32-7ebe9a4b45db" :question/math-academy-id "q-348289" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $u={x}^{3}+1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that ${x}^{3}=u-1.$ \n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **$u$** | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe can now solve the integral using our new variable $u,$ remembering to use the new limits of integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{6{x}^{5}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{({x}^{3}+1)^{2}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2(u-1)}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{u}-\\frac{2}{{u}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+\\frac{2}{u})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\frac{2}{2})-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73575143-4460-538c-8aa1-056786aecc43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317962} :answer-field/id #uuid "8176f5f0-47d6-5f0f-ba47-63f839d87ff0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a0c50af-6c83-593f-8570-9f596da85472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348295" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+1,\\,y={t}^{3}+4.$ Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{2}t,\n$$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3}{2}t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{3}{2}t)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,2}=\\frac{3}{4(2)}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94afa6f7-9ec3-5674-aaf4-34a2e1a3c792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{192\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318073} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e9a7e53-8eec-5495-85b5-fb5492802a0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "88eff08e-1bbb-529f-965a-96596e4595fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-348298" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=6t-2{t}^{3},\\,y=6{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2{t}^{3})=6-6{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2})=12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(6-6{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(12t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36-72{t}^{2}+36{t}^{4}+144{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $36{t}^{4}+72{t}^{2}+36,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{36({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}\\cdot 6\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =72\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =72\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =72\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70131d8e-e110-5e84-bcff-45651751b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317953} :answer-field/id #uuid "805f7f24-9fb6-5e8f-8459-868c76a93452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab4d806a-76c8-563a-a10d-c7fabb39c7b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-348304" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319251 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "304ba8a2-d6e2-5916-8283-099a63b493ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317712} :answer-field/id #uuid "949120a2-97b4-5bb0-a7f5-ce16e986ff6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1d29f74-3ab5-58b3-9701-b4401085ff7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348350" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-5}+\\frac{B}{(x-5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+3=A(x-5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5+3 & =A(5-5)+B \\\\ B & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+3 & =A(0-5)+8 \\\\ 3 & =-5A+8 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+3}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x-5}+\\frac{8}{(x-5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-5}\\,\\text{d}x+8{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-\\frac{8}{x-5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0-5|)-(\\frac{8}{1-5}-\\frac{8}{0-5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)-(-2-\\left(-\\frac{8}{5}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)+\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caa5522d-84dd-5929-b7e4-41606f76d20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236} :answer-field/id #uuid "645da014-7ccd-5611-b0a7-bd19b901c215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23a998c2-d0ee-57a9-9649-0245890af7bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-348426" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{(x+5)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x+5)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+5)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5+1 & =A(-5+5)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+5)-4 \\\\ 1 & =5A-4 \\\\ 5A & =5 \\\\ A & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x+1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{x+5}-\\frac{4}{(x+5)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{4}{x+5}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+5|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+5|)+(\\frac{4}{1+5}-\\frac{4}{0+5}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)+(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)-\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65e9975c-1bdc-5628-a25b-036ad5ef4f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317914} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5ecd49-16d3-5305-8c3e-09eec361ecd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9f15bc0-efc2-57a6-80e1-e0ac9f6a8276" :question/math-academy-id "q-348473" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x-4}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $x(x-4)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n8-x=A(x-4)^{2}+Bx(x-4)+Cx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and $C$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-0 & =A(0-4)^{2}+B(0)(0-4)+C(0) \\\\ 8 & =16A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-4 & =A(4-4)^{2}+B(4)(4-4)+C(4) \\\\ 4 & =4C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ and substituting the known values of $A$ and $C,$ we can solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-1 & =A(1-4)^{2}+B(1)(1-4)+C(1) \\\\ 7 & =9A-3B+C \\\\ 7 & =9\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-3B+1 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8-x}{x(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2x}-\\frac{1}{2(x-4)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|\\,{|}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x-4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-(-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd5ee984-6d80-5e81-b647-b2efb1b7c50a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318411} :answer-field/id #uuid "88591a36-5e22-59fa-9309-9ac0822bfa60" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5ae593e0-851a-5fb0-b188-6070b7e5f252" :question/math-academy-id "q-348521" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's first factor the denominator of the integrand:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{{x}^{2}-12x+36}=\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x-6}+\\frac{B}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A$ and $B,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(x-6)^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n2-x=A(x-6)+B.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=6,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-6 & =A(6-6)+B \\\\ B & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $B,$ we can solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-0 & =A(0-6)-4 \\\\ 2 & =-6A-4 \\\\ A & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2-x}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}(-\\frac{1}{x-6}-\\frac{4}{(x-6)^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{x-6}\\,\\text{d}x-4{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{(x-6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-6|\\,{|}_{2}^{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-6}\\,{|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)+4(-1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+4\\left(-\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f28c9eb-3840-5b5a-a56c-67005ceedbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317708}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317708} :answer-field/id #uuid "686eae4d-b0d6-58e1-ba44-ec76b61e6ca2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f8cb3ef-802b-56b0-aecd-f648c03fc650" :question/math-academy-id "q-348528" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+8 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+8 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+8 \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x=6{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{1}{|x+3|\\sqrt{(x+3)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\sqrt{2}-3$ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}-3}^{-1}\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+8}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} (\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457b53c0-5c6d-516d-a877-c712b70b39e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317791} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d3deba-cbdd-5184-8055-2e4be419e417" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "935ea227-3967-5286-b590-f5c1cff1d8bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-348530" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+3 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+3 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+3 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x=3{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{1}{|x+2|\\sqrt{(x+2)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-2+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{0}\\frac{3}{|x+2|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9281596-87b0-57d8-a581-4893a3a5f6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229} :answer-field/id #uuid "480d7b82-80ba-5bf8-83a2-2e84505b516f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6c4d7d65-4e55-5a11-a6e7-c436fd955297" :question/math-academy-id "q-348531" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+24 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+({5}^{2}-{5}^{2})+24 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2})-25+24 \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x=8{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{1}{|x-5|\\sqrt{(x-5)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-5).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $5+\\sqrt{2}$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5+\\sqrt{2}}^{7}\\frac{8}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+24}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} (\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =8(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221653f2-bbc5-5c48-bf4c-c1771f86560d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317671} :answer-field/id #uuid "d553147c-43b1-5c52-9661-80a3cc7c948a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8adadcfb-a209-54ca-845d-63966459ad0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-348532" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{4+\\sqrt{2}}^{6}\\frac{12}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+15 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+15 \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4+\\sqrt{2}}^{6}\\frac{12}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=12{\\int }_{4+\\sqrt{2}}^{6}\\frac{1}{|x-4|\\sqrt{(x-4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4+\\sqrt{2}$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4+\\sqrt{2}}^{6}\\frac{12}{|x-4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =12{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2} \\\\  & =12(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} (\\sqrt{2})) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3df062f-a483-5b34-b334-45ecc7d4e171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318270}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318270} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9736f4f-99a2-5e67-8623-ab108ae29d34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7023621-9b53-549a-bf91-15f518a7082a" :question/math-academy-id "q-348533" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the expression inside the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+15 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+15 \\\\  & =(x+4)^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x=5{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{1}{|x+4|\\sqrt{(x+4)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-4+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $-2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4+2/\\sqrt{3}}^{-2}\\frac{5}{|x+4|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+8x+15}}\\,\\text{d}x & =5{\\int }_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2}\\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\,\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,u\\,{|}_{2/\\sqrt{3}}^{2} \\\\  & =5(\\operatorname{arcsec} (2)-\\operatorname{arcsec} \\,\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d659652f-4675-56ba-94dc-9e4c7d51f754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318277} :answer-field/id #uuid "477d5e91-a032-5984-8576-8ecd87b3defd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "300d8b9a-8fa6-522c-8c63-eee3475f0ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-348553" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{\\sqrt{3}-3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+10 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+10 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+10 \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x+3)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+3).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $\\sqrt{3}-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{\\sqrt{3}-3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+6x+10}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "778a582d-ea0f-55e8-bf04-1f6f33d24848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317974} :answer-field/id #uuid "64e67d9c-4cdd-54b3-9e59-0a2243fd10d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9ae8ff40-323b-5106-b233-450ffba3e186" :question/math-academy-id "q-348554" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4x+5 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+({2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-4+5 \\\\  & =(x-2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x-2)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x-2).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}-4x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a387894e-1aa9-5b94-892e-fdc3e5365b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318127}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318127} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5c6ce3-3264-5d12-918a-88126d369913" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3d03c752-2f01-5331-91ed-02e39cb4137e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348555" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{-3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+8x+17 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+({4}^{2}-{4}^{2})+17 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2})-16+17 \\\\  & =(x+4)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{(x+4)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(x+4).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nLet's use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}+8x+17}\\,\\text{d}x & =6{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =6(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c473cf-9a6d-5243-afee-2a9a22ea295c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} :answer-field/id #uuid "02cb7d9f-15cb-5de3-b16f-f7c775c110ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318428